import{_ as s,P as o,d as r,e as c,f as n,H as l,q as i,y as a}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging","xkChars":2,"xkWords":4158,"xkLessons":{"intro-19-appendix-c":{"title":"Appendix C Common method kernel and Interdisciplinary Bridging","lead":"The shared core shares methodological responsibilities without taking away each text’s authority over definitions or letting two texts manufacture independent empirical support for one another.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 4827–4830; original site location: this part’s introduction (no independently numbered anchor). See the theory-v3.0 source for the current full text; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for download and checking against the original edition.","The introduction states that interoperability across texts can share methodological responsibilities while each text retains its authoritative definitions and way of being used. What is shared is how grounds and boundaries are accounted for, not a requirement that all books use the same chapter structure, number of outputs, or numbering. [T-intro]","“Does not make one text independent empirical evidence for the other” concerns source independence: if A cites B and B cites A in return, without new observations of an object, this cannot count as two external validations. Texts interpreting one another may have interpretive value, but material status must be retained. [T-intro]","Interoperability requires at least declarations of the current text version, cited definitions, corresponding content, and differences. Similar names do not mean identical definitions; the same example may also come from the same upstream source, and changing format does not make sources independent. [T-intro]","This topic has only the user-provided Xikari original and has not obtained the authoritative full text of World Model; it can therefore explain only this book’s proposed interoperability responsibilities, without claiming to have completed an item-by-item comparison of the two books or approved the other book’s definitions. [T-intro]","C.1 below is a list of stable references at the navigation level; it can tell readers what to compare, but does not mean comparison has been completed. Actual joint adoption still requires checking the respective boundaries of definitions, evidence, norms, and real-world authorization. [T-intro; T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["First fix the identities and versions of both texts.","Separate shared methods from each text’s definitions and the way it is used.","Do not count restatements from the same source as independent empirical evidence.","When the other text is missing, retain unverified status rather than inventing a comparison."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Shared responsibilities","leftText":"Checks of evidence, baselines, boundaries, and so on may be shared.","rightTitle":"Uniform outputs","rightText":"The introduction does not mandate identical chapter structures, numbering, or counts."},{"leftTitle":"Texts corroborating one another","leftText":"Can interpret each other’s claims.","rightTitle":"Independent empirical evidence","rightText":"Requires independent object materials; circular citation does not produce it."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: two texts cite the same constructed simulation and claim two independent experiments.","Boundary in the original: the shared core cannot cancel different texts’ authoritative definitions and the ways they are used."],"questions":["Does sharing a core require uniform numbering?","Can another text serve as an independent experiment?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/figures/core-intro.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"intro-19-appendix-c","start_line":4884,"end_line":4887,"startLine":4884,"endLine":4887}],"outputId":"intro-19-appendix-c","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: circular citation does not increase the sample","text":"Constructed text A records a fictional case; text B rewrites it and is then cited by A. The material lineage still returns to the same internal illustration and cannot be recorded as two independent real-world experiments.","assumptions":"Both texts and the case are teaching constructions.","boundary":"The interpretive use of restatement is not denied; only its empirical status is limited."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: two texts cite the same constructed simulation and claim two independent experiments.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-intro"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: the shared core cannot cancel different texts’ authoritative definitions and the ways they are used.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-intro"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does sharing a core require uniform numbering?","answer":"No. The introduction explicitly does not require identical chapter structures, output counts, or numbering.","basis":"Separate method from implementation."},{"question":"Can another text serve as an independent experiment?","answer":"Another text is not independent merely because it is another document; material lineage and status must be checked.","basis":"Cross-citation adds no evidence."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/figures/core-intro.png","alt":"Share methodological responsibilities while each text preserves its definitions and the way it is used; cross-citation does not become independent empirical evidence.","caption":"Based on the introduction to Appendix C."}],"diagramBrief":"One figure distinguishes shared responsibilities, separate definitions, and source independence.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This introduction discusses the status of interoperability across texts and offers no external historical case; the two-text teaching construction is explicitly fictional."},"openQuestions":["The authoritative full text of World Model was not obtained, and no actual item-by-item comparison of the two books was performed."],"taskId":"S-C-1","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.1, Chinese source lines 4360–4370","claim_support":["Six-row table of stable references for the methodological core."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-intro","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§intro-19-appendix-c, Chinese source lines 4356–4359","claim_support":["Shared methods do not unify artifacts or produce independent empirical evidence."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-method","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.17–4.17.4, Chinese source lines 959–980","claim_support":["Three shared methodological responsibilities."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-diff","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.4, Chinese source lines 4397–4402","claim_support":["Cross-text differences and minimum shared semantics."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"附录C 共同方法内核与跨学科桥接; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/source/S-C-1.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"57b62bf2264394088a5e7244899761b67bd9b716d4651cfab0a7cfe328d5b515","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.1":{"title":"C.1 Methodological Core and Stable References","lead":"A stable reference first locates the relevant level, then returns to the original definitions and conditions; the six-row table does not merge different materials and responsibilities into one kind of evidence.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 4831–4841; original site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-1. See the theory-v3.0 source for the current full text; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for download and checking against the original edition.","The definition level covers objects, states, feedback, scale, circles, information status, and recasting, pointing to this framework’s definitions and World Model’s authoritative definitions. A reference at the definition level means consulting word meanings and how objects are defined; definitions cannot establish that an object actually exists. [T-main]","Methodological constraints Laws 1–3 point to §4.17, with separate responsibilities for tasks and representations, material status, and facts and action eligibility. Citing a constraint can explain which checks should be made, but cannot establish that following it necessarily improves real-world outcomes. [T-main; T-method]","Conditional formal propositions F point to §4.18–4.19 and this appendix: task recovery, closure, common actions, transformation composition, error, and dynamic refinement. Formal proofs must carry their assumptions; their results are separate from whether real objects satisfy those assumptions. [T-main]","The four empirical questions point to the pre-registration of formal tests in Part Four: grouping, channels, historical increments, and representational closure, respectively. Stable references need not only the name of the question but also the current specific test and its eligibility. Historical increments are not a common threshold for all learning. [T-main]","The twelve conclusion rules point to the individual conclusion rules in Part Four, used for records of outcome statuses, mechanism composition, and bridges to normative procedures. A seemingly matching story cannot replace a conclusion rule, nor can formal rigor alone authorize real actions. [T-main]","Governance invariants concern finalizing before results, evidence, baselines, authorization, protection, and lifecycle, pointing to the two texts’ existing governance provisions. This is a shared checking agenda given by this book, not verified identical provisions or execution results in an actual version of the other text. [T-main]","Stable references should preserve original namespaces and locations, rather than use only similar words such as “method” or “closure.” Mapping an item in A to an item in B requires recording invariants, differences, unknowns, and stopping conditions. The other full text was not read for this topic, so no such mapping is fabricated. [T-main; T-diff]"],"argumentSteps":["Determine each row’s role: definition, method, formal proposition, empirical claim, conclusion rule, or governance.","Use the body-text locations in the table to read the actual conditions.","Trace empirical references further to specific tests and source material.","Keep unverified cross-text differences unverified; shared names cannot fill them in."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Definition","leftText":"Specify objects and terms.","rightTitle":"Empirical","rightText":"Test whether specific objects satisfy a claim."},{"leftTitle":"Formal proposition","leftText":"Provides mathematical conclusions within assumptions.","rightTitle":"Real-world choice","rightText":"Also requires factual applicability, values, and authority."},{"leftTitle":"Stable reference","leftText":"Can be traced to the exact definitions and conditions.","rightTitle":"Matching words","rightText":"Similar terminology cannot replace an actual mapping."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: directly determining that two sets of definitions are completely identical because the other text uses the same word “closure.”","Boundary in the source: a shared citation layer does not turn one text into independent empirical support for another."],"questions":["Is the six-row table six empirical laws?","Without the full text of World Model, can interoperability be declared complete?","Why can a formal proof not directly establish policy effectiveness?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/figures/core-references.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.1","start_line":4888,"end_line":4898,"startLine":4888,"endLine":4898}],"outputId":"section-c-1","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: trace a citation to its role","text":"An artificial review says, “See F; therefore this city’s policy is effective.” First check that F is a conditional formal proposition, supporting derivation under assumptions. Whether city data satisfy those assumptions and whether the policy is effective remain independent empirical tasks; the identifier F cannot fill those gaps.","assumptions":"The review and city policy are fictional, illustrating only a check of citation status.","boundary":"No conclusion is drawn about real policy, and no unread external theorem is introduced."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: directly determining that two sets of definitions are completely identical because the other text uses the same word “closure.”","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: a shared citation layer does not turn one text into independent empirical support for another.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main","T-intro"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Is the six-row table six empirical laws?","answer":"No. It lists six kinds of citation responsibilities and locations with different roles.","basis":"Navigation classification."},{"question":"Without the full text of World Model, can interoperability be declared complete?","answer":"No. Only this book’s requirements and unverified correspondences can be reported.","basis":"Material scope."},{"question":"Why can a formal proof not directly establish policy effectiveness?","answer":"Whether real objects satisfy the premises, and the empirical results, still need independent testing.","basis":"Separate form from applicability."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/figures/core-references.png","alt":"The six stable-reference categories separately check definitions, methods, formal propositions, empirical tests, conclusion rules, and governance.","caption":"Based on the six-row table in C.1; no claim is made that an actual mapping to the other book has been verified."}],"diagramBrief":"One diagram locates the six rows in layers of definitions, methods and formal propositions, empirical tests and conclusion rules, and governance.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section provides citation navigation between two texts. The other authoritative text was not supplied; empirical or comparative results must not be fabricated."},"openQuestions":["World Model’s definitions and system of outputs have not been independently checked; actual correspondences await availability of the source."],"taskId":"S-C-1","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.1, Chinese source lines 4360–4370","claim_support":["Six-row table of stable references for the methodological core."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-intro","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§intro-19-appendix-c, Chinese source lines 4356–4359","claim_support":["Shared methods do not unify artifacts or produce independent empirical evidence."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-method","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.17–4.17.4, Chinese source lines 959–980","claim_support":["Three shared methodological responsibilities."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-diff","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.4, Chinese source lines 4397–4402","claim_support":["Cross-text differences and minimum shared semantics."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.1 方法内核与稳定引用; Chinese source lines 4831–4841; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/source/S-C-1.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"b2047513a04abab73268165090f3596f5420e39bd7f18f584ff1c8fb12bb520f","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.2":{"title":"C.2 Common method constraints","lead":"Methods 1–3 specify responsible model use; following the procedure does not itself establish a model’s real-world effectiveness.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4842–4847, original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-2. The current full text is in the theory-v3.0 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","Method 1 declares task, representation, horizon, environment, operations, and error together. Task specifies what must be recovered or predicted; representation specifies what the model can see; horizon and environment limit the valid domain; operations specify permitted actions; and error defines what counts as good enough. Stating an accuracy rate alone while omitting these conditions may mix different tasks. [T-main; T-full]","Static recovery, dynamical closure, and intervention sufficiency are tested separately. A representation that recovers today’s target need not update to tomorrow; predicting passive evolution does not guarantee that actions chosen under that representation are feasible for every hidden state. These are different questions, not something summarized by “the model is good enough.” [T-main; T-full]","Method 2 requires preservation of material identity, source provenance, inferential premises, and support scope. Real observations, internal teaching examples, simulations, normative claims, and formal proofs do not all become external empirical evidence because the same report contains them. Several charts processing the same upstream observation are not several independent materials either. [T-main; T-full]","At minimum, support scope must be traceable to object, window, and corresponding proposition. Summaries or secondary texts may be the material actually read, but must not stand in for verification of the complete source. Inferential premises should also be listed explicitly to prevent a conclusion from circulating back as its own evidence. [T-main; T-full]","Method 3 requires facts, value judgments, and action eligibility to hold separately. An action’s possible effectiveness does not establish that it is worth doing; a worthwhile goal does not establish effective means or an actor’s authority to execute them. Conversely, lacking execution authority does not prohibit grounded normative recommendations, but those recommendations must not be treated as execution authorization. [T-main; T-full]","These three are responsibilities for model use, not three empirical laws of the world. Whether following them reduces errors or adds unnecessary burdens requires separate evaluation, especially with a specified task and comparison baseline. Tool-completion rates cannot replace outcome testing. [T-main]","Teaching should record “checks complete,” “formal derivation correct,” “real-world premises satisfied,” and “actual net benefit established” separately. This section introduces methods and explicitly artificial examples only; it reports no effectiveness of this framework in any real institution. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Declare task, representation, horizon, environment, operations, and error together.","Separate tests of static recovery, dynamical closure, and intervention.","Preserve material identity and upstream provenance; repeated processing is not independent empirical evidence.","List the respective grounds for facts, normative premises, and execution eligibility.","Evaluate the method’s actual benefits and burdens; compliance does not self-certify effectiveness."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Static recovery","leftText":"The current target is determined by the current representation.","rightTitle":"Dynamical closure","rightText":"The updated representation can still be updated from the present representation."},{"leftTitle":"Repeated processing","leftText":"Multiple presentations of the same material.","rightTitle":"Independent empirical evidence","rightText":"Requires actually independent supporting sources, not changes of format."},{"leftTitle":"Worth doing","leftText":"Has public normative reasons.","rightTitle":"I may execute it","rightText":"Also requires specific valid authorization and procedures."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: one simulation produces a chart, summary, and video, claimed as three independent real-world validations.","Teaching counterexample: accurately predicting an outcome is taken to establish one’s authority to impose the action.","Boundary in the source: the three methods are responsibility requirements, not empirical laws that compliance guarantees effectiveness."],"questions":["Why can a model not be summarized by “accurate” alone?","Do different charts from one source count as independent validation?","Does lacking execution authority prohibit recommendations?","Can compliance with the three methods prove the framework effective?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-2/figures/methods-three.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.2","start_line":4899,"end_line":4904,"startLine":4899,"endLine":4904}],"outputId":"section-c-2","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: statically sufficient, dynamically unclosed","text":"Let an artificial state be x=(a,b), representation π(x)=a+b, and current task q=a+b, exactly recovered by g(y)=y. Define one-step evolution F(a,b)=(2a,b). States (1,0) and (0,1) both have current representation 1, but their representations after evolution are 2 and 1. The current sum alone cannot supply one exact next sum for both.","assumptions":"The integer states and specified deterministic evolution are entirely mathematical constructions, not social data.","boundary":"Rejects only exact closure of this representation for this dynamic task; it does not reject its static-sum task."},{"title":"Teaching construction: effects and authority are separate","text":"An artificial scheduling recommendation may reduce waiting, but changes employees’ shifts. Effects need evidence about the object; fairness rankings need normative reasons; execution still needs specific scheduling authority and applicable procedures. One prediction cannot supply all three.","assumptions":"Fictional scheduling; no real effect is claimed.","boundary":"No scheduling is implemented, and no consent is given on employees’ behalf."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: one simulation produces a chart, summary, and video, claimed as three independent real-world validations.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: accurately predicting an outcome is taken to establish one’s authority to impose the action.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: the three methods are responsibility requirements, not empirical laws that compliance guarantees effectiveness.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why can a model not be summarized by “accurate” alone?","answer":"Tasks, horizons, environments, and operations differ, as do static, dynamic, and intervention sufficiency.","basis":"Method 1."},{"question":"Do different charts from one source count as independent validation?","answer":"They are not independent upstream sources; their respective processing uses can only be described separately.","basis":"Method 2."},{"question":"Does lacking execution authority prohibit recommendations?","answer":"Recommendations can still be offered under grounded normative premises, but are not execution permission.","basis":"Method 3."},{"question":"Can compliance with the three methods prove the framework effective?","answer":"No. Benefits and additional burdens need separate real-world evaluation.","basis":"The original text’s second paragraph."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-2/figures/methods-three.png","alt":"Method 1: six conditions of task and representation; Method 2: source and identity; Method 3: separate facts, values, and authority.","caption":"Based on C.2 and 4.17."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-2/figures/methods-dynamics.png","alt":"Two states both sum to 1; after the specified evolution their sums are 2 and 1. Static recovery does not imply closure.","caption":"Teaching mathematical construction."}],"diagramBrief":"The two diagrams distinguish the responsibilities of the three methods and the artificial static/dynamic counterexample.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section concerns methodological responsibilities and mathematical distinctions, claiming no external empirical effects. It uses reproducible artificial examples and does not fabricate empirical cases."},"openQuestions":["This section has not evaluated the three methods’ costs or effects in real tasks."],"taskId":"S-C-2","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.2, Chinese historical source lines 4371–4376","claim_support":["The three method constraints and the boundary requiring separate evaluation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-full","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.17–4.17.4, Chinese source lines 959–980","claim_support":["Full statement of the three methods and separation of responsibilities."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.2 共同方法约束; Chinese source lines 4842–4847; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-2/source/S-C-2.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"8b084b23cedbefef14395b4c6f330a4d4882de4c4dff47f46e72a593c70bae8d","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.3":{"title":"C.3 Shared Governance Invariants","lead":"The nine safeguards protect versions, null conclusions, evidence identity, division of responsibility, parsimony, residuals, lifecycle, and low-power positions. They cannot offset one another through a total score.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4848–4869, original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-3. The current full text is in the theory-v3.0 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","Invariant one, “Finalize the record before results appear, and after they appear only append to it,” protects traceable differences between prior rules and later revisions. After results are seen, a separate new version of tasks, thresholds, and windows may be set up, but the old version must not be overwritten and presented as the original. Revision does not prohibit learning; append-only versioning enables honest comparison. [T-main]","Invariant two, “Lack of support for a positive claim does not amount to support for the null model, that is, a comparison model without the claimed factor,” preserves null-conclusion boundaries. Insufficient samples, inapplicable measurements, conflicts, or uncertain outcomes may block a positive conclusion without proving no effect. A null model also needs support through its own test. [T-main]","Invariant three prohibits definitions, conventions, internal teaching examples, and internal simulations from serving on their own as external empirical support. They may clarify logic, test implementation, and reveal counterexamples, but “this holds in the stipulated model” differs from a real object actually being that way. [T-main]","Invariant four treats material identity, proposition role, output status, and missing-data status as mutually independent dimensions. External observation is a material identity; empirical hypothesis is a proposition role; draft/finalized is an output status; and not collected/conflicting is a missing-information type. A record may have these values simultaneously; one overall “confirmed” label cannot cover them all. [T-main]","Invariant five separates responsibilities for sourcing, modeling, foresight, evaluation, authorization, and execution. One report may contain several modules, but a real source does not automatically provide predictive eligibility, and completed evaluation does not automatically provide execution authority. Every role transition needs its own grounds. [T-main]","Invariant six requires model complexity to undergo proportionate simplification, deletion, and necessity review. Predictive or empirical capability should be compared against simple baselines and independent evaluation material. Formal proofs, textual explanations, and normative arguments are checked according to their tasks, without a compulsory out-of-sample accuracy measure. Simplicity is not inherently correct either; the question is whether added complexity accomplishes the claimed task. [T-main]","Invariant seven: “Residuals generate only competing candidates. They do not automatically generate variables in reality.” Prediction residuals may arise from omitted mechanisms, measurement, noise, changing objects, or a wrong model. A new name without discriminating material must not turn error into an entity. Retain multiple candidates and their failure conditions. [T-main]","Invariant eight binds capability to domain, validity period, drift, suspension, restoration, and retirement. Past effectiveness does not guarantee admissibility in a new environment. Suspension or retirement does not mean deleting historical records; restoration also cannot merely change a label while downstream users retain the old effects. [T-main]","Invariant nine preserves low-power positions, privacy, protective nondisclosure, appeals, and exit; aggregate results cannot flatten them. Missing voices may reflect unavailability for protective reasons and must not be filled in as consent. Average improvement cannot erase harm and rights questions at particular positions either. [T-main]","The nine safeguards jointly constrain model use; they cannot be compressed into a single “governance qualification score.” Passing one does not fill another’s gap. Independently grounded local conclusions may remain, but strong judgments depending on missing premises must stop at the corresponding boundaries. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Finalize before results, preserving differences through appended revision versions.","Distinguish unsupported positive claims from support for a null model.","Record material identity, proposition role, output status, and missing-data status separately.","Separate responsibilities from sourcing through execution, checking each one’s required evidence and authority.","Review complexity by task, and list competing explanations for residuals.","Continuously examine domain and lifecycle, preserving protective nondisclosure and exit."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Positive claim unsupported","leftText":"Current material is insufficient to support this claim.","rightTitle":"Null model supported","rightText":"A different conclusion requiring an independent test."},{"leftTitle":"Internal simulation","leftText":"Model output under stipulated settings.","rightTitle":"External empirical evidence","rightText":"Material about real objects, not something simulation automatically becomes."},{"leftTitle":"Prediction evaluation","leftText":"Compare baselines and independent evaluation material.","rightTitle":"Evaluation of formal proof","rightText":"Check assumptions and derivation; do not impose out-of-sample accuracy."},{"leftTitle":"Residual candidate","leftText":"Explanations awaiting discrimination.","rightTitle":"Real-world variable","rightText":"Requires additional material; naming does not create an object."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: changing thresholds after seeing results, overwriting the old version, and still claiming to have finalized in advance.","Teaching counterexample: no significant improvement is taken as proof of absolutely no effect.","Teaching counterexample: every normative argument without a test set is declared invalid.","Boundary in the source: aggregate benefits cannot erase protection and appeals for low-power positions."],"questions":["Can a model be changed after results?","Does orthogonality of the four dimensions mean statistical independence?","Does every argument need out-of-sample metrics?","Does nondisclosure mean evidence does not exist?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/figures/invariants-nine.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.3","start_line":4905,"end_line":4926,"startLine":4905,"endLine":4926}],"outputId":"section-c-3","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: four labels on one record","text":"An artificial record is labeled “external interview transcript” for material identity, “empirical mechanism candidate” for proposition role, “draft” for output status, and “key time point not collected” for missing-data status. All four can hold simultaneously. External identity neither supports the draft conclusion automatically nor removes the time gap.","assumptions":"The record is entirely fictional; the four-dimensional example does not replace a complete enumeration.","boundary":"Draft status does not negate the material’s value; the restriction concerns a specific inference."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a residual does not directly create a new entity","text":"An artificial prediction is 10, observation 12, and residual 2. This may arise from measurement bias, omitted inputs, or incorrect model form. Naming 2 “invisible drive” adds no discriminating evidence. Propose explanations that can fail separately and obtain additional material.","assumptions":"The values and candidate explanations are teaching constructions; 12−10=2.","boundary":"Does not deny that omitted factors may exist in reality; it rejects identification accomplished by naming alone."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: changing thresholds after seeing results, overwriting the old version, and still claiming to have finalized in advance.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: no significant improvement is taken as proof of absolutely no effect.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: every normative argument without a test set is declared invalid.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: aggregate benefits cannot erase protection and appeals for low-power positions.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a model be changed after results?","answer":"A revised version may be appended; old records must not be erased to disguise later success as prior success.","basis":"Invariant one."},{"question":"Does orthogonality of the four dimensions mean statistical independence?","answer":"Here it means distinct dimensions of recording cannot be merged, not that they are probabilistically independent.","basis":"Typing responsibility in Invariant four."},{"question":"Does every argument need out-of-sample metrics?","answer":"No. Evaluation of predictive empirical capability differs from evaluation of formal, textual, or normative tasks.","basis":"Invariant six."},{"question":"Does nondisclosure mean evidence does not exist?","answer":"No. Protective unavailability and its inferential limits must be retained.","basis":"Invariant nine."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/figures/invariants-nine.png","alt":"The nine safeguards separately cover versions, null conclusions, material, division of responsibilities, parsimony, residuals, lifecycle, and protection; they do not substitute for one another.","caption":"Based on the nine items in C.3."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/figures/invariants-dimensions.png","alt":"Material identity, proposition role, output status, and missing-data status are four recording dimensions.","caption":"A teaching record demonstration, not a claim of statistical independence."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/figures/invariants-residual.png","alt":"Observation 12, prediction 10, residual 2; competing candidates involving measurement, omission, model form, and others await discrimination.","caption":"An artificial calculation, not an empirical discovery."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams cover the nine safeguards, four-dimensional records, and an artificial residual counterexample.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section gives shared governance rules without claiming historical implementation or utility. Explicit artificial records and residual calculations illustrate boundaries on misuse."},"openQuestions":["No empirical evaluation is supplied showing that the governance rules actually reduce errors or costs."],"taskId":"S-C-3","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.3, Chinese historical source lines 4377–4396","claim_support":["All nine governance invariants and boundaries on task evaluation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.3 共同治理不变量; Chinese source lines 4848–4869; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/source/S-C-3.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"aad987768bcca616f1dce03b23799da8af3338d3cef80bbe6e338a5ea07c165a","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.4":{"title":"C.4 Preserving Differences Across Texts","lead":"Interoperability must preserve differences. Similar names, similar cases, or complete tables cannot replace actual correspondence between both sides’ definitions and rules.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4870–4875, original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-4. The current full text is in the theory-v3.0 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","The first set of checks listed here covers proposition layering, the pre-registration of tests of the four empirical questions, null conclusions, scale closure, noncommutation tests, and inferential premises. They respectively control what a claim is, how it is tested, what cannot be inferred, whether transformations preserve a task, whether order matters, and which premises a conclusion depends on. [T-main]","The second group of checks covers information identity, systems of outputs, unknowns classified by type, experimental evaluation, capability evidence, and model lifecycles. These govern where material comes from, how records are organized, how missingness is distinguished, whether evaluation is independent, whether capabilities have empirical support, and when to pause or retire a model. Similar terms do not guarantee identical semantics or thresholds. [T-main]","“The complete definitions and rules of one text cannot be replaced by something with a similar name, or by a case, from the other” means that mapping must examine conditions, outputs, and ceilings, rather than finding a similar picture. If correspondence is incomplete, preserve explicit differences and “unknown” instead of assuming equivalence; interoperability also need not force changes to the other side's definitions. [T-main]","The source explicitly does not force alignment between this framework's nine scale transformations and the transformation steps commonly used in World Models, nor merge this framework's scale transformation record covering fourteen aspects with the other text's closed system of outputs. No one-to-one or many-to-one correspondence is inferred from the numbers here. The other authoritative full text was unavailable for this discussion, so no claim is made to have checked its actual items or counts. [T-main]","The minimum shared semantics are, item by item: preservation—which distinctions remain; change—which properties are recast; folding—which differences are merged; omission—which items are not carried over; new unknowns—which uncertainties arise after conversion; source genealogy—how material was passed along; domain of application—under which objects and conditions it is valid; residuals—what remains unexplained; and stopping—when it can no longer be used. Different outputs may answer these common questions separately. [T-main]","More unification is not necessarily better interoperability. Forced unification can conceal different unknown states or support thresholds; no communication at all loses opportunities for shared checks. A practical approach lists the source text’s definitions, the target text’s definitions, verified preservation, unverified differences, and stopping points item by item. This discussion provides only a constructed demonstration, not a purported actual mapping between the two books. [T-main]","Source genealogy and unresolved status must accompany downstream references: if upstream material states only a candidate, using another system's item name downstream cannot upgrade it to confirmed. Format mapping expands neither the domain of application nor authority; actual adoption is still checked under each side's own definitions and rules. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["List both versions and the exact locations of their definitions side by side.","Check the 12 responsibilities in the two groups individually; names are no substitute.","The nine scale transformations and the record of fourteen aspects each retain their own organization.","Use preservation/change/folding/omission/unknowns/genealogy/domain/residuals/stopping as minimum common questions.","Explicitly preserve differences where items cannot correspond, limiting downstream conclusions."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Similar names","leftText":"Words appear similar.","rightTitle":"Correspondence of definitions","rightText":"Premises, objects, outputs, and restrictions have actually been checked."},{"leftTitle":"Minimum shared semantics","leftText":"Shareable questions of responsibility.","rightTitle":"Merging outputs","rightText":"This section explicitly does not require it."},{"leftTitle":"Incomplete mapping","leftText":"Correspondence is unknown or differs.","rightTitle":"Assumed equivalence","rightText":"Cannot automatically fill a material gap."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: A says “unknown” and B says “pending review”; they are treated as the same state without checking meanings.","Teaching counterexample: deleting the other system's unique conditions to force a nine-to-nine mapping.","Boundary in the source: the scale transformation record covering fourteen aspects cannot be directly merged with another closed system of outputs."],"questions":["Why not unify the formats directly?","Do identical unknown labels have identical meanings?","Can a comparison be declared complete without the other full text?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-4/figures/difference-contracts.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.4","start_line":4927,"end_line":4932,"startLine":4927,"endLine":4932}],"outputId":"section-c-4","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: preserving protective unknowns","text":"Constructed system A separates “unavailable for privacy reasons” from “not yet collected”; B has only one blank cell. Importing both as blank loses the boundary on authority to obtain information. The original missingness type and use restrictions can be appended separately, or mapping can remain incomplete; a blank cannot be treated as permission to continue collection.","assumptions":"A and B are fictional systems and do not correspond to actual items in World Models.","boundary":"This only demonstrates checks for semantic loss; no personal data is actually read."},{"title":"Teaching construction: counts cannot determine a mapping","text":"A constructed source scheme has nine operations, while a target process has six steps. The numbers 9 and 6 neither prove interoperability impossible nor determine how to merge items. Each operation's inputs, conditions, outputs, and failure boundaries need comparison.","assumptions":"The target's six steps are entirely fictional; the book's count of nine scale transformations comes from the source.","boundary":"No actual mapping of operations between the two books was completed."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: A says “unknown” and B says “pending review”; they are treated as the same state without checking meanings.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: deleting the other system's unique conditions to force a nine-to-nine mapping.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: the scale transformation record covering fourteen aspects cannot be directly merged with another closed system of outputs.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why not unify the formats directly?","answer":"Identical formats may conceal differences in definitions; sharing minimum semantics does not require identical outputs.","basis":"The second paragraph."},{"question":"Do identical unknown labels have identical meanings?","answer":"Check missingness types, acquisition authority, and downstream effects, not names alone.","basis":"Typed unknowns."},{"question":"Can a comparison be declared complete without the other full text?","answer":"No. Only this book's requirements and differences awaiting verification can be listed.","basis":"Material limitations for this discussion."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-4/figures/difference-contracts.png","alt":"Separately check propositions, empirical material, null conclusions, closure, noncommutativity, and dependencies, alongside identity, outputs, unknowns, evaluation, capabilities, and lifecycles.","caption":"Based on the two groups of checks in C.4."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-4/figures/difference-minimum.png","alt":"Nine shared responsibilities: preservation, change, folding, omission, new unknowns, genealogy, domain of application, residuals, and stopping.","caption":"Based on C.4's final sentence, not a unified output."}],"diagramBrief":"Two diagrams distinguish checks of definitions from nine minimum semantic questions; no unverified one-to-one mapping is drawn.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section concerns cross-text methodological boundaries. The other authoritative full text was not obtained; no actual mapping or historical case is fabricated."},"openQuestions":["Actual definitions, outputs, and transformation steps in World Models await its authoritative full text before correspondence can be established."],"taskId":"S-C-4","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.4, Chinese source lines 4397–4402","claim_support":["Two groups of correspondence checks, artifact differences, and nine minimum shared semantics."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.4 跨文本的差异保留; Chinese source lines 4870–4875; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-4/source/S-C-4.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"95b6383275b2c9790b6d74725356d4e12e85848bfe918a0890aa0d61e567c329","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.5":{"title":"C.5 Methodological Examples of Mathematical Scale Bridging","lead":"Mathematical bridging must carry objects, mappings, initial values, history, errors, and validity periods. Approximate commutation of one statistic cannot be upgraded into equivalence of entire states.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4876–4886; original site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-5. For the current full text, see the theory-v3.0 source; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Let x be a microscopic state, M a coarse-graining map, F_t the microscopic evolution, and F̄_t the macroscopic evolution. The two paths are microscopic evolution followed by mapping, M(F_t(x)), and mapping followed by macroscopic evolution, F̄_t(M(x)). Approximate commutation for the corresponding task can be discussed only if their difference does not exceed ε under specified initial conditions, parameter domains, norms, and time intervals. [T-main]","“The corresponding task” is crucial. If M preserves only a sum, the commutation check concerns the sum's dynamics, not recovery of each component; different source states with the same sum may legitimately be merged. Agreement on one statistic cannot establish equivalence of the entire object; conversely, it does not follow that every compression necessarily harms the current task. [T-main]","A proof must also specify the order of limits, error control, and validity period. Holding at each fixed short time does not mean holding uniformly over an infinite time horizon; taking a parameter to zero and time to infinity may require different arguments. This discussion offers only finite discrete constructions and does not claim to prove any general theorem on exchanging limits. [T-main]","Bridging should carry eight responsibilities together: source object, target object, mapping, scaling law or limit, initial values, retained history, error, and failure boundaries. Two models may use different representations; the key is to explain what is preserved for the current task and what cannot be inferred. [T-main]","A mathematical proof holding, a real object satisfying its premises, and choosing an actual option are three distinct responsibilities. The formal existence of a macroscopic map does not establish it as the actual mechanism of a social object, still less confer intervention authority. Losses in social recasting are not physical entropy either. Applicable quantities and models must be established separately; names alone cannot borrow conservation laws or units. [T-main]","The source's research entries 05.05 “Gödel, Incompleteness, and Computability,” 05.06 “Effective Theories, Scale Closure, and Coarse-Graining,” and 05.08 “Model Selection, Parsimony, and Expressive Power” provide navigation only. Specific conclusions require returning to the original works' assumptions, proofs, and validity domains. These research packages or original works were not obtained here, so no claim is made to have checked their theorems or to use their bibliography as support for social bridging. [T-main]","Appendix D's domain map likewise preserves specialist terminology, measurement, arguments, and boundaries. Teaching examples can illustrate only the structure of conditional counterexamples, not impersonate implemented experiments or actual cases. Topic numbers are not rankings of theoretical importance. [T-main]","The two reproducible constructions below test only paths, statistics, and time windows. Diagram symbols come from this section, while numerical values and update rules are constructed for teaching. What can be proved is the arithmetic of these two models, not processes in actual cities, organizations, or minds. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["State the source and target objects, M, F_t, F̄_t, and the task.","Fix the initial-value domain, parameters, error norm, and validity period.","Calculate the difference between the two paths and specify the only statistic being controlled.","Check extrapolation over time or limits; narrow the domain or rebuild the model on failure.","Separate the three responsibilities of proof, real-world premises, and option/authorization.","Research numbers provide navigation only; do not claim unread original works as verified."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Approximate commutation","leftText":"The difference between two paths is controlled within a specified domain and window.","rightTitle":"Full-state equivalence","rightText":"Requires all target distinctions; one statistic is insufficient."},{"leftTitle":"Short-time bound","leftText":"A finite validity window.","rightTitle":"Long-term conclusion","rightText":"Requires new uniform control or a corresponding proof."},{"leftTitle":"Holds under mathematical assumptions","leftText":"A derivation for formal objects.","rightTitle":"Real-world premises are satisfied","rightText":"Requires measurement mappings and domain evidence."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: preserving the sum is used to declare that every person's distributional state is identical.","Teaching counterexample: acceptable error in the first three steps automatically promises acceptable error indefinitely.","Boundary in the source: bibliographies or research numbers cannot replace checking original assumptions and proofs.","Boundary in the source: losses in social recasting cannot directly be treated as physical entropy."],"questions":["Does commutation of two paths allow recovery of every microscopic state?","Is ε a universal measure of social information loss?","Can research numbers replace checking the original works?","Can mathematical proof directly decide a real-world option?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-5/figures/bridge-square.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.5","start_line":4933,"end_line":4942,"startLine":4933,"endLine":4942}],"outputId":"section-c-5","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: exact commutation of sums does not recover components","text":"Take x=(a,b), M(a,b)=a+b, F(a,b)=(b,a), and macroscopic F̄(y)=y. M(F(x))=b+a=a+b=F̄(M(x)), so the paths commute exactly at every step. But (1,0) and (0,1) both sum to 1, and the representation cannot distinguish which component is 1.","assumptions":"Ordered pairs of real numbers, swap dynamics, and the sum task are constructed mathematical definitions.","boundary":"This proves closure only for the sum task, not recoverability of the full state or lossless real-world resource distribution."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a short-window tolerance cannot automatically be extended","text":"Let integer t≥0, F_t(a,b)=(a+0.1t,b), let M take the sum, and let F̄_t(y)=y, with absolute-value error. The difference between the two paths is always 0.1t. Over t=0,1,2,3, the maximum is 0.3, satisfying ε=0.3. At t=4, 0.4>0.3, so the original short-window conclusion cannot extend to the fourth step.","assumptions":"Entirely constructed discrete evolution, with 0.1 treated as exactly one tenth and no measurement noise.","boundary":"This demonstrates a validity window, not a claim that all coarse-graining accumulates loss or fails in the long term."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: preserving the sum is used to declare that every person's distributional state is identical.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: acceptable error in the first three steps automatically promises acceptable error indefinitely.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: bibliographies or research numbers cannot replace checking original assumptions and proofs.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: losses in social recasting cannot directly be treated as physical entropy.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does commutation of two paths allow recovery of every microscopic state?","answer":"No. Examine what M and the task preserve; a sum may remain while components become indistinguishable.","basis":"The statistic's boundary."},{"question":"Is ε a universal measure of social information loss?","answer":"No. It belongs to a given norm, task, parameter domain, and time window.","basis":"A local error condition."},{"question":"Can research numbers replace checking the original works?","answer":"No. Numbers only guide navigation; restrictions must be stated when original works are unread.","basis":"The third paragraph."},{"question":"Can mathematical proof directly decide a real-world option?","answer":"No. Real-world premises and actual choice separately carry evidentiary and normative-authority responsibilities.","basis":"The three responsibilities."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-5/figures/bridge-square.png","alt":"Compare two paths: microscopic x through F_t then M, versus x through M then macroscopic F̄_t.","caption":"A general schematic form, not a claimed social theorem."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-5/figures/bridge-window.png","alt":"Constructed error 0.1t: the first three steps are ≤0.3; the fourth step reaches 0.4 and exceeds the limit.","caption":"An exact rational-number teaching example."}],"diagramBrief":"A two-path diagram and a constructed time-window diagram show mapping, order, and error limits.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section is explicitly a general mathematical-method example, not a claimed social theorem or actual experiment. It uses complete, independently constructed, reproducible examples without pretending to have read the original works behind the research numbers."},"openQuestions":["No actual social object has been verified as satisfying these mathematical premises.","The original works in research packages 05.05/05.06/05.08 were not obtained, so no factual assertions about their specific theorems are made."],"taskId":"S-C-5","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.5, Chinese source lines 4403–4413","claim_support":["General mathematical examples of scale, eight bridging responsibilities, and limitations of research navigation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.5 数学尺度桥接的方法示例; Chinese source lines 4876–4886; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-5/source/S-C-5.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"97f29372c7994a2351b66ed49b24ea1733be501bc1ae86b099eb8284a724001d","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.6":{"title":"C.6 Task Recovery and Worst-Case Error (F1)","lead":"If states in a representation fiber require different answers, the representation alone cannot recover the task exactly. With error permitted, the answer depends on output space, loss, and distribution.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4887–4904; original site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-6. For the current full text, see the theory-v3.0 source; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","First list assumptions: X is nonempty, π:X→Y is deterministic with Y=π(X), and the target is q:X→V. The fiber X_y={x:π(x)=y} contains all source states merged by the representation. The decoder may read only y, without secretly inspecting the source state, identity labels, or undeclared history. The image-set condition ensures every y has a nonempty fiber. [T-main]","Task-recovery proposition: a g:Y→V satisfying q=g∘π exists if and only if q is constant on every fiber. Necessity: if π(x)=π(x′)=y, then q(x)=g(y)=q(x′). Sufficiency: for each y, define g(y) as the common q value of its fiber. Constancy ensures no ambiguity, and substituting any x gives q(x)=g(π(x)). [T-main]","Uniqueness holds only on the actual image Y: every y comes from at least one x, so q(x) fixes every feasible g. If the target representation space is artificially extended to points with no source state, the theorem does not constrain those points. Existence as a set function also does not guarantee always-halting computability on infinite domains or sufficient finite budgets. [T-main]","The finite scalar worst-case-error proposition additionally requires finite X, real-valued q, any real number as a permitted output, and loss defined as worst absolute error over the full domain. Let a_y and b_y be each fiber's minimum and maximum. The optimal risk is (1/2)max_y(b_y−a_y), not a universal formula for all losses. [T-main]","Lower-bound proof: for any output c, |a_y−c|+|b_y−c|≥b_y−a_y, so at least one endpoint has error ≥(b_y−a_y)/2. Every decoder's full-domain risk is at least the largest half-range. Attainability proof: choose c=(a_y+b_y)/2 on each fiber. All target values lie between the endpoints, each error is ≤the half-range, and equality holds at the endpoints. Thus the lower bound is attained, and the infimum is actually achieved. [T-main]","Given ε≥0, a decoder with full-domain error ≤ε exists if and only if every fiber's range is ≤2ε. Infinite domains require suprema and boundedness checks; vectors, restricted outputs, average loss, and probability weights are different optimization problems. The midpoint is one optimal choice; “the exact-recovery g is unique” must not be transferred into “a globally optimal worst-error g must be unique.” [T-main]","The binary version separately assumes uniformly distributed finite source states and zero-one loss, with each fiber's two class counts n_y0/n_y1. Output 0 makes n_y1 errors, and output 1 makes n_y0 errors; majority choice yields min(n_y0,n_y1). Total risk is Σ_y min(n_y0,n_y1)/|X|. Randomized labels produce only a convex combination of the two error counts and cannot do better; ties permit multiple optimal rules. [T-main]","Uniformity applies to source states, not fibers or representation values. Fibers of different sizes have different weights. Even exact arithmetic describes only this given constructed distribution, not error frequencies in an actual population. Without a reasonable mapping and sample, neither half-ranges nor error rates should be attached to real people. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix X, π, the actual image Y, and the current task q.","Check whether q is constant on each fiber, proving necessity and sufficiency.","If error is permitted, first state the output space and loss.","For scalar worst-case loss, use the endpoint lower bound and midpoint construction.","For binary uniform zero-one loss, count majority decisions fiber by fiber.","Separate existence, uniqueness, computability, obtainability, and real-world applicability."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Exact recovery","leftText":"The target is constant on each fiber, and g is unique on the image.","rightTitle":"Approximate optimum","rightText":"Optimized under a loss, not automatically unique."},{"leftTitle":"Real-valued output","leftText":"The midpoint may be available.","rightTitle":"Integer output","rightText":"Restrictions on the midpoint can change the optimal radius."},{"leftTitle":"Uniform source states","leftText":"Every x has equal weight.","rightTitle":"Uniform representation values","rightText":"Large and small fibers receive equal weight: a different distribution."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: the decoder secretly reads the states' identity labels and then claims recovery from the representation alone.","Teaching counterexample: applying the optimal real-valued midpoint formula to integer outputs without checking.","Boundary in the source: a set function's existence does not guarantee computability on infinite domains or sufficient budgets.","Boundary in the source: constructed uniform error rates do not estimate actual population incidence."],"questions":["Why must Y equal the actual image?","Why is the midpoint optimal?","Can random guessing outperform majority choice?","Does the formula apply to arbitrary vectors or average losses?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/figures/recovery-fibres.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.6","start_line":4943,"end_line":4960,"startLine":4943,"endLine":4960}],"outputId":"section-c-6","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: the half-range lower bound and output restrictions","text":"Take three constructed states a,b,c, with π(a)=π(b)=u, π(c)=v, and target q=(2,7,10). The u fiber has endpoints 2 and 7; output 4.5 gives both errors as 2.5. Output 10 on v gives error 0, so the full-domain optimal risk is 2.5. ε=2 is impossible, while ε=2.5 is feasible. If outputs must be integers, choosing 4 or 5 on u gives worst error 3, and 2.5 can no longer be reported.","assumptions":"A finite domain, specified scalar target, and worst absolute loss; all values are constructed.","boundary":"The numbers are not a universal measure of social information loss; integer constraints define a different problem."},{"title":"Teaching construction: majority classes and source weights","text":"Take 7 constructed, equally weighted source states. The u fiber has labels 0,0,1, and the v fiber has 0,1,1,1. Output 0 on u makes 1 error; output 1 on v makes 1 error. The optimal error rate is 2/7≈28.5714%. Incorrectly giving each fiber weight 1/2 produces (1/3+1/4)/2=7/24, changing the distribution.","assumptions":"Entirely teaching binary states, with zero-one loss and equal source-state weights.","boundary":"This proves risk only under this finite distribution, not actual frequencies."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: the decoder secretly reads the states' identity labels and then claims recovery from the representation alone.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: applying the optimal real-valued midpoint formula to integer outputs without checking.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: a set function's existence does not guarantee computability on infinite domains or sufficient budgets.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: constructed uniform error rates do not estimate actual population incidence.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why must Y equal the actual image?","answer":"It ensures nonempty fibers and makes the target uniquely determine exact g on all of Y.","basis":"Existence and uniqueness."},{"question":"Why is the midpoint optimal?","answer":"The endpoint triangle inequality gives the half-range lower bound; the midpoint makes all targets in the fiber satisfy that upper bound.","basis":"A two-step proof."},{"question":"Can random guessing outperform majority choice?","answer":"Not in this binary zero-one-loss problem with a fixed distribution; its error is a convex combination of the two pure-label errors.","basis":"The specified conditions."},{"question":"Does the formula apply to arbitrary vectors or average losses?","answer":"No. Another optimization proof for the corresponding space and loss is required.","basis":"Applicability boundaries."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/figures/recovery-fibres.png","alt":"States with the same representation must have the same target for exact recovery; existence and computability remain separate.","caption":"Based on the task-recovery proposition in C.6."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/figures/recovery-radius.png","alt":"Endpoints 2 and 7 have midpoint 4.5, real-valued radius 2.5, and integer radius 3.","caption":"A constructed scalar example."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/figures/recovery-binary.png","alt":"One error in the 3-state fiber plus one error in the 4-state fiber gives risk 2/7 under equal weighting of source states.","caption":"A constructed binary example, not a real-world error rate."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams show the fiber criterion, scalar endpoints/midpoints, and binary counts; all numerical values are teaching constructions.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section gives formal proofs for set functions and finite loss. Constructed numbers support recalculation; historical cases neither replace theorems nor masquerade as experiments."},"openQuestions":["Computability on infinite domains and various other losses lie outside the assumptions of this topic's propositions."],"taskId":"S-C-6","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.6, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4414–4431","claim_support":["All of the task-recovery equivalence and uniqueness proofs, scalar worst-case error, and binary formulas."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.6　任务恢复与最坏误差（F1）; Chinese source lines 4887–4904; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/source/S-C-6.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"b33e4349621c01ad80039d63a9528eca33a75e0374c5e1444d45edc4cc71dcd0","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.7":{"title":"C.7 Composition of Conversions and Actual Execution (F4)","lead":"Each step completing its own local task does not guarantee preservation of a common endpoint; representational insufficiency and an incorrect actual rule can also coexist.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4905–4914; original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-7. See the theory-v3.0 source for the current full text; the historical Chinese research manuscript and slices remain downloadable in their original versions for checking.","Let f:X→Z and h:Z→W, with the final task q already recoverable from the intermediate representation, so q=g∘f. The proposition “Task Preservation through Successive Conversions” (§4.18.4) asks whether the composition h∘f still allows recovery of the same q, not whether the second step successfully answers a different question. [T-main]","The necessary and sufficient condition is that g is constant on every fiber of h restricted to the actual image f(X). Necessity: if q=k∘h∘f, then for any reachable z=f(x) and z′=f(x′) with h(z)=h(z′), we have g(z)=q(x)=k(h(z))=k(h(z′))=q(x′)=g(z′). [T-main]","Sufficiency: for each reachable final value w, define k(w) as the common g value on its reachable intermediate fiber. Constancy makes this unambiguous, and substitution gives q=k∘h∘f. The theorem concerns only f(X); unreachable intermediate values need not satisfy the same constraint. Do not turn empty labels in the codomain into additional empirical requirements. [T-main]","The source's Boolean-pair counterexample: X={0,1}², f retains both positions, h retains only their sum, and q asks for the first position. h merges (0,1) and (1,0) into 1, yet they require answers 0 and 1. The first step preserves and the second counts successfully in their own terms, but the same final positional question is not preserved. [T-main]","Adding the Boolean value of the first position makes q recoverable without recovering every source detail. If the final task is instead changed directly to counting, that is a different successful task, not a repair of the original q. Evaluation cannot change the question after the fact and count the original task as passed. [T-main]","Separate representational sufficiency from correctness of the execution rule: for three source states a/b/c, f=(0,0,1) and q=(0,1,1), while the actual g_actual outputs 0 for both representations 0/1. States a/b have the same representation but different q, witnessing representational insufficiency. The fiber containing c unambiguously requires answer 1, so the actual output 0 independently witnesses a rule error. The output 0 for a is still correct in this instance; failures elsewhere cannot erase that local correctness. [T-main]","Changing g_actual to (0,1) repairs only c and cannot simultaneously satisfy the conflict between a/b. This decomposition depends on complete knowledge of source representations, targets, and the actual rule. In reality, if internal inputs or rules are unknown, retain the gaps rather than pretending every failure has a unique classification. [T-main]","An original remaining in existence and a reviewer obtaining it in time are different conditions. Looking back at the original after an error does not establish availability before action. Formal recovery, rule implementation, access timing, and permissions require separate arguments; existence in storage cannot be treated as actual input. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix the same final q and first check q=g∘f.","Check whether g is constant on h fibers only within reachable f(X).","Prove both directions and check whether a changed question is being passed off as a repair.","Separate representational conflicts from rule errors on distinguishable states.","Preserve local correctness and internal unknowns, and check accessibility at the time."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Successful local counting","leftText":"The second step completes its own summation task.","rightTitle":"Recovery of the endpoint position","rightText":"The original q must still be recoverable from the final representation."},{"leftTitle":"Insufficient representation","leftText":"The same representation requires different answers.","rightTitle":"Execution error","rightText":"The actual rule is wrong even where the representation is sufficient to distinguish the required answer."},{"leftTitle":"The original exists","leftText":"Stored material can be revisited.","rightTitle":"Available in time","rightText":"Authorized access early enough before action requires separate evidence."}],"boundaries":["Source counterexample: retaining only the sum after preserving two positions cannot answer the first-position question.","Source counterexample: three states can exhibit both a representational conflict and an independent rule error.","Teaching counterexample: changing the final question to counting while claiming the original positional task has been repaired.","Source boundary: unknown internal representations or rules preclude forcing a unique failure classification."],"questions":["Why check only the actual image?","Why can two individually successful stages still fail?","Can changing a rule remedy every representational insufficiency?","Does recovering the original afterward establish sufficiency beforehand?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-7/figures/composition-bits.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Real case · Mars Climate Orbiter: differences in units at an interface","href":"/en/cases/mars-climate-orbiter"}],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.7","start_line":4961,"end_line":4970,"startLine":4961,"endLine":4970}],"outputId":"section-c-7","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Recalculating the source's mathematical construction: four Boolean states","text":"(0,0)→sum 0→first position 0; (0,1)→sum 1→first position 0; (1,0)→sum 1→first position 1; (1,1)→sum 2→first position 1. The sum-1 fiber has a conflict, so no exact first-position decoder reading only the sum exists. Adding the first-position bit restores the required target.","assumptions":"A constructed state domain and query in the user's source, not an actual process incident.","boundary":"This counterexample is not generalized into inevitable failure of all compositions."},{"title":"Recalculating the source's mathematical construction: the ceiling on rule repair","text":"The old rule outputs 0 for a/b/c, with correctness in the order correct/incorrect/incorrect. Changing the output for representation 1 to 1 gives 0/0/1, with correctness correct/incorrect/correct. c is repaired, but b remains wrong. Changing the output for representation 0 to 1 would make a wrong and b correct; it cannot make both correct simultaneously.","assumptions":"f=(0,0,1), q=(0,1,1), and the rules are all explicitly constructed models.","boundary":"This is not an algorithm for uniquely attributing real failures; unknown inputs must remain unknown."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source counterexample: retaining only the sum after preserving two positions cannot answer the first-position question.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source counterexample: three states can exhibit both a representational conflict and an independent rule error.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: changing the final question to counting while claiming the original positional task has been repaired.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: unknown internal representations or rules preclude forcing a unique failure classification.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why check only the actual image?","answer":"Unreachable intermediate values have no source states, so final-task recovery need not be constrained by them.","basis":"Scope of the proposition “Task Preservation through Successive Conversions.”"},{"question":"Why can two individually successful stages still fail?","answer":"The two stages may complete different local tasks without conditions preserving a common q.","basis":"Responsibility for the same endpoint."},{"question":"Can changing a rule remedy every representational insufficiency?","answer":"No. When the same representation requires different answers, any single-output rule must get at least one wrong.","basis":"Fiber conflict."},{"question":"Does recovering the original afterward establish sufficiency beforehand?","answer":"No; accessibility, timeliness, and lawfulness before action require separate evidence.","basis":"Timing of access."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-7/figures/composition-bits.png","alt":"Among the four Boolean pairs, sum 1 includes first-position values 0 and 1, so the final q is not recoverable.","caption":"Recalculation of the source's constructed counterexample."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-7/figures/composition-errors.png","alt":"The old rule is correct/incorrect/incorrect; the new rule is correct/incorrect/correct, while the representational conflict remains.","caption":"Recalculation of the source's three-state mathematical construction."}],"diagramBrief":"The two diagrams list the Boolean-pair conflict and changes to the three-state rule; both recalculate mathematical constructions in the source.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section provides formal propositions and explicitly constructed counterexamples without disguising them as actual execution incidents."},"openQuestions":["Real failures require evidence of internal inputs, rules, and access at the time; responsibility cannot be assigned from this formal example alone."],"taskId":"S-C-7","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.7, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4432–4441","claim_support":["The proof of F4 in both directions, the reachable image, both counterexamples, and restrictions concerning access timing."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.7　转换的接续与实际执行（F4）; Chinese source lines 4905–4914; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-7/source/S-C-7.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"264eb1c5d2ca42069da8d324687c6a854efa08ed59f78b122b8e37b5f18c0d68","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.8":{"title":"C.8 Closure and error propagation (F2, F5)","lead":"Exact closure requires consistent projected successors for every merged state; approximate closure additionally requires stepwise errors, amplification constants, and valid regions to remain applicable.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4915–4926; original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-8. See the theory-v3.0 source for the current full text; the historical Chinese research manuscript and slices remain downloadable in their original versions for checking.","Let deterministic one-step dynamics be F:X→X, and let representation π:X→Y use its actual image. There exists F̄ such that π∘F=F̄∘π if and only if x/x′ with the same π still have the same π after updating. Necessity follows because a macroscopic function must give the same output for the same input. For sufficiency, define F̄(y) on each fiber as the common π(F(x)). This applies C.6 with target q replaced by π∘F. [T-main；T-fibre]","If the condition holds throughout the domain, then π∘F^t=F̄^t∘π for every nonnegative integer t. At step zero both sides equal π. Assuming the statement at step t, π∘F^{t+1}=F̄∘π∘F^t=F̄^{t+1}∘π, completing the induction. Holding only in observed samples does not automatically establish validity throughout the domain for arbitrary steps. [T-main]","With allowed actions U, closure must be checked for every explicit F_u before stepwise composition for any prespecified action sequence. Also check whether source states in the same fiber can all implement the macroscopic action, or restrict the available scope. If the same macroscopic action corresponds to different microscopic implementations, consistency of their macroscopic outcomes also requires checking. The same action name does not imply the same implementation map. [T-main]","A finite stochastic system uses a transition kernel P(x,x′). If, for any x/x′ in the same source block, the sums of transition probabilities into each target block B are equal, define the macroscopic kernel by that common value. Sufficiency: even if conditioning on the macroscopic present and past produces different within-block distributions, a weighted average of a constant probability for the same target block remains that constant. The macroscopic one-step update therefore does not depend on the hidden mixture. [T-main]","If the same macroscopic one-step law is required for all initial distributions, equality block by block is also necessary. Start separately from point masses at x and x′ in the same block: the macroscopic present is identical and must have the same distribution over the next block, so the probability sums for every target block must be equal. Preserving only the next-step mean or coinciding by chance under one initial mixture does not satisfy this all-initial-distributions requirement. [T-main]","For the approximate case, let the target space have metric d. Suppose the one-step projection defect d(π(F(x)),F̄(π(x)))≤ε holds for all reached x, and F̄ satisfies a Lipschitz bound L in the region containing the relevant comparison points. Starting from the same initial value y_0=π(x_0), let e_t be the difference between the actual projection and the iterated macroscopic state. The triangle inequality splits the next error into the one-step defect ε and the old deviation amplified macroscopically by Le_t, giving e_{t+1}≤ε+Le_t. [T-main]","Induction from e_0=0 gives e_1≤ε, e_2≤ε(1+L), and e_t≤εΣ_{j=0}^{t−1}L^j. Here L≥0; when L≠1 the sum is (L^t−1)/(L−1), and when L=1 it is t. For L<1 there is a uniform bound ε/(1−L), but this still presupposes continued validity of the domain and stepwise conditions. A large upper bound does not directly prove actual divergence. [T-main；T-bound]","If ε and L vary over time, use the recurrence e_{t+1}≤ε_t+L_t e_t rather than continuing to apply the fixed-parameter formula. Average fitting error is not a per-state upper bound. If a trajectory leaves the working region or a new intervention is outside U, the original guarantee stops where its conditions fail. [T-main]","Exactness, approximation, empirical applicability, and normative usability remain separate. Stochastic closure under ordinary distributions need not cover interventions; a common mathematical update does not establish that an action is feasible or authorized. The kernels, numerical values, and constants below are teaching constructions, not real domain measurements. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Check whether deterministic successors have consistent projections within each representation fiber.","Use step zero and induction to prove finite-step commutation throughout the domain.","For each action, check F_u, feasibility, and microscopic implementations sharing a name.","For stochastic kernels, check probability sums into each target block, distinguishing all initial distributions from a particular mixture.","For approximation, check stepwise ε, regional L, and the same initial value, then apply the recurrence.","Stop using the original bound when drift, new actions, or failure of the valid region invalidates it."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Equal means","leftText":"Only one statistic agrees.","rightTitle":"Stochastic closure","rightText":"Probability sums into every target block agree."},{"leftTitle":"One-step average fit","leftText":"Loss averaged over a sample or distribution.","rightTitle":"Per-state ε bound","rightText":"The defect is controlled at every reached state."},{"leftTitle":"An expanding error upper bound","leftText":"The guarantee may weaken.","rightTitle":"Actual divergence","rightText":"This does not follow directly from a loose upper bound alone."},{"leftTitle":"Identical action labels","leftText":"The names are the same.","rightTitle":"Consistent microscopic implementation","rightText":"Outcomes and feasibility conditions require checking."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: different states in the same block have different probabilities of reaching a target block, yet one common kernel is used.","Teaching counterexample: checking only passive dynamics and then claiming closure for every intervention.","Source boundary: time-varying ε/L cannot be inserted directly into the fixed-constant formula.","Source boundary: mathematical propositions cannot determine empirical satisfaction of premises or normative usability."],"questions":["Why does exact one-step closure extend to multiple steps?","Must stochastic paths within a block be exactly identical?","Does L<1 guarantee real-world safety forever?","Does an upper bound of 0.7 mean the error is 0.7?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/figures/closure-exact.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.8","start_line":4971,"end_line":4982,"startLine":4971,"endLine":4982}],"outputId":"section-c-8","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: check block probabilities, not identical internal paths","text":"Take constructed states a,b,c with blocks A={a,b} and B={c}. The transition row for a is (1/2,0,1/2), for b (0,1/2,1/2), and for c (0,0,1). Although a/b have different internal destinations, both send probability 1/2 to A and B, giving macroscopic A→(1/2,1/2) and B→(0,1). If b's row changes to (0,3/4,1/4), point-mass starts at a/b give different next-step probabilities of B, 1/2 and 1/4; a common kernel for all initial distributions fails.","assumptions":"A finite constructed Markov kernel; every row sums to 1, with no external empirical evidence.","boundary":"The claim concerns only the stated blocks and kernel; equal means or a particular initial mixture are not evidence for all initial distributions."},{"title":"Teaching construction: different amplification of the same one-step defect","text":"Fix constructed ε=0.1 and e0=0. The three-step upper bound is 0.1(1+0.5+0.25)=0.175 when L=0.5; 0.3 when L=1; and 0.1(1+2+4)=0.7 when L=2. These are upper bounds. No actual trajectories are specified, so the three actual errors cannot be claimed to equal these numbers exactly.","assumptions":"The constants and premises are stipulated for teaching, with the relevant regional conditions assumed to remain satisfied.","boundary":"This is not empirical fitting, and growth of the L=2 upper bound does not imply divergence of every trajectory."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: different states in the same block have different probabilities of reaching a target block, yet one common kernel is used.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: checking only passive dynamics and then claiming closure for every intervention.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: time-varying ε/L cannot be inserted directly into the fixed-constant formula.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: mathematical propositions cannot determine empirical satisfaction of premises or normative usability.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does exact one-step closure extend to multiple steps?","answer":"The domain-wide condition permits reusing the commutation relation at each step, establishing induction.","basis":"The entire domain is not a sample."},{"question":"Must stochastic paths within a block be exactly identical?","answer":"No, but the total probabilities of reaching every target block must be equal.","basis":"The blockwise condition."},{"question":"Does L<1 guarantee real-world safety forever?","answer":"No. The domain, per-state bounds, and actual normative conditions must still hold.","basis":"Boundaries between mathematics and reality."},{"question":"Does an upper bound of 0.7 mean the error is 0.7?","answer":"No. It guarantees only that the error does not exceed it when the premises hold; the error may be smaller.","basis":"A bound versus an actual value."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/figures/closure-exact.png","alt":"Same representation, same successor representation; induction gives multiple steps throughout the domain; check each action separately.","caption":"Based on the proposition “Closure under Dynamics and Admissible Interventions” in C.8."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/figures/closure-random.png","alt":"Closure holds when a/b each send 1/2 to A and B; changing b→B to 1/4 causes failure for all initial distributions.","caption":"A constructed finite kernel."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/figures/closure-error.png","alt":"e下一步≤ε+Le (next-step error ≤ ε+Le); the three-step values 0.175/0.3/0.7 are upper bounds for different L.","caption":"Constructed fixed constants, not measured errors."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams show exact closure, a constructed stochastic block kernel, and the error recurrence; full proofs appear in the main text.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section formally proves deterministic/stochastic closure and metric error results. Numerical values come from explicitly constructed mathematical kernels and bounds, without claiming actual experiments."},"openQuestions":["Per-state errors, available actions, and working regions of real models require domain validation."],"taskId":"S-C-8","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.8, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4442–4453","claim_support":["All of the necessary-and-sufficient proofs for deterministic/stochastic closure and the error recurrence."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-fibre","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.6, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4414–4431","claim_support":["Fiberwise constancy and the existence of a function."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-bound","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"Chinese source, §4.18.5, lines 1001—1004","claim_support":["The three cases of L and per-state bounds."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.8　闭合与误差传播（F2、F5）; Chinese source lines 4915–4926; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/source/S-C-8.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-8/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"645f26bc500cb7270a193896c281f08d9b47eda708a03f4ca067c0eb9d0724cd","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.9":{"title":"C.9 Differences between tasks in a two-queue system","lead":"The same mean representation can answer total-quantity questions exactly while remaining insufficient for local overload. Adding the maximum repairs some tasks, but does not guarantee closure under location-specific interventions or higher-dimensional dynamics.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4927–4942; original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-9. See the theory-v3.0 source for the current full text; the historical Chinese research manuscript and slices remain downloadable in their original versions for checking.","This is an explicitly constructed model in the user's source: the ordered coordinates (q₁,q₂) each range over 0…30, giving 31²=961 states. The local-overload target is max(q₁,q₂)>15. Only for calculating expected error, give the 961 source states equal weight; the means are not equally weighted, and this is not a real queue distribution. [T-main]","The mean m=(q₁+q₂)/2 maps both (0,20) and (10,10) to 10; the former is overloaded and the latter is not, so exact classification is impossible. Partitioning into fibers by the integer sum s is equivalent to partitioning by the mean, merely avoiding decimal bookkeeping. [T-main]","For s=16…30, there are s+1 states in total. The number with q₁>15 is s−15, and likewise for q₂>15. Both cannot occur simultaneously in this range, so the overloaded count is 2(s−15). The nonoverloaded count is (s+1)−2(s−15)=31−s. For s≤15 all states are nonoverloaded; for s>30 all are overloaded. Thus only the middle fibers conflict. [T-main]","Choose the majority label in each fiber. For s=16…20, overloaded states are the minority, giving minimum errors 2(s−15), which sum to 2(1+2+3+4+5)=30. For s=21…30, nonoverloaded states are the minority, summing to 10+9+…+1=55. The optimal total is 85 errors; dividing by 961 gives 8.844953…%. Independent recalculation of this finite enumeration yields 85 without simulation-based sampling approximation. [T-main；T-binary]","The rule ‘report overload only when the mean>15’ is equivalent to s>30. It misses all overloaded states within s=16…30, totaling 2(1+…+15)=240. There are no false positives, because mean>15 necessarily implies local>15. Its 155=240−85 additional errors above the optimum are excess risk of this rule relative to the optimum; all 240 cannot be attributed to unavoidable representational loss. [T-main]","If the target changes to recovering maximum length, then for a given s the smallest possible maximum is ceil(s/2) and the largest is min(30,s). The greatest range width, 15, occurs at s=30. States (0,30) and (15,15) have maxima 30 and 15, and the real-valued midpoint 22.5 gives worst-case error 7.5; no other fiber has a larger width. Requiring integer outputs gives radius 8 by choosing 22 or 23 for this fiber. Changing the output space changes the problem. [T-main；T-binary]","Add the maximum M: overload is recovered through M>15 and maximum length directly from M, but the larger position remains unknown. With exactly two queues, the smaller value=2m−M. If each position serves at most c items per round, there are no arrivals, and c≥0, then M′=(M−c)_+ and m′=[(M−c)_++(2m−M−c)_+]/2. Thus (m,M) is closed under the specified symmetric model without recovering positional order. [T-main]","Changing position-specific capacities to (15,10) gives (0,20)→(0,10) and (20,0)→(5,0). Both initial representations (m,M) are (10,20), but their successors are (5,10) and (2.5,5), so closure fails. What changes is the symmetry condition of the dynamics; this is not simply a claim that the maximum is useless. [T-main]","Three-queue counterexample: (20,20,5) and (20,15,10) both have mean 15 and maximum 20. After serving 15 at each position, they become (5,5,0) and (5,0,0), with totals 10 and 5 and means 10/3 and 5/3. The mean plus maximum no longer determines the other two values, so the two-queue closure result cannot extend unconditionally to higher dimensions. [T-main]","Retain both positive and negative results: a total-quantity representation could already answer total-quantity questions exactly; adding task-relevant variables can repair some insufficiencies; the same repair does not guarantee tasks involving different operations, positions, or dimensions. Defining success in every task as recovery of all microscopic details would erase the bounded successes that actually hold here. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix the 961 constructed ordered states and the equally weighted source distribution.","Partition into fibers by sum s and calculate overloaded/nonoverloaded counts.","Compare the optimal majority decision's 85 errors with the threshold rule's 240.","For maximum recovery, separately check worst-case radii for real/integer outputs.","After adding M, check symmetric two-queue dynamics, then examine position-specific capacities and higher-dimensional counterexamples.","Retain positive tasks such as total-quantity recovery; do not generalize local failure into total loss."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Optimal representational risk","leftText":"85/961 is the best classification risk with this information.","rightTitle":"Actual-rule risk","rightText":"The threshold rule gives 240/961, including 155 additional errors."},{"leftTitle":"Adding M","leftText":"Repairs local-overload and maximum-length tasks.","rightTitle":"Recovering position","rightText":"Which queue is larger remains unknown."},{"leftTitle":"Symmetric service for two queues","leftText":"(m,M) suffices for updating.","rightTitle":"Position-specific capacity or three queues","rightText":"The premises change, requiring closure to be checked again."}],"boundaries":["Source counterexample: (0,20)/(10,10) have the same mean but different local-overload status.","Source counterexample: capacities (15,10) give different successors for the same (m,M).","Source counterexample: three queues with the same mean and maximum have different totals after service.","Teaching counterexample: calling all 240 rule errors unavoidable information loss."],"questions":["Is 85 a real system's error rate?","Why are the 240 errors not all information loss?","What remains missing after M is added?","Is requiring all details for success reasonable?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/figures/queues-counts.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.9","start_line":4983,"end_line":4998,"startLine":4983,"endLine":4998}],"outputId":"section-c-9","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Exact enumeration of the source construction: 85 and 240","text":"Generate q1,q2 from 0 to 30 one by one; group by q1+q2, then sum the smaller of the two class counts in every group to obtain 85. Comparing ‘sum>30’ with ‘maximum>15’ state by state gives 240, a difference of 155. The classification risk is 85/961≈8.844953%.","assumptions":"Enumeration of the entire constructed state domain with equally weighted source states, not real-world sampling.","boundary":"The numbers belong only to the specified task domain and distribution; they cannot establish missed-report rates in real service institutions."},{"title":"Recalculation of the source’s constructed dynamics: identical summaries, different successors","text":"With position-specific capacities 15/10, the initial states (0,20)/(20,0) both have (m,M)=(10,20). After updating, they become (0,10)/(5,0), with summaries (5,10)/(2.5,5). Thus one function depending only on the summary cannot produce both successors.","assumptions":"Artificial deterministic dynamics with no arrivals and fixed position-bound capacities.","boundary":"Does not negate the original closure conclusion under equal capacity c."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source counterexample: (0,20)/(10,10) have the same mean but different local-overload status.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source counterexample: capacities (15,10) give different successors for the same (m,M).","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source counterexample: three queues with the same mean and maximum have different totals after service.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: calling all 240 rule errors unavoidable information loss.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Is 85 a real system's error rate?","answer":"No. It is the optimal error count over 961 equally weighted artificial states; actual distributions require separate evidence.","basis":"Distribution boundary."},{"question":"Why are the 240 errors not all information loss?","answer":"This representation already admits a rule making only 85 errors; the extra 155 are additional risk from rule choice.","basis":"Separate representation from rule."},{"question":"What remains missing after M is added?","answer":"It does not retain which position is larger; position-specific interventions and three-queue dynamics may still require other distinctions.","basis":"Task-relative sufficiency."},{"question":"Is requiring all details for success reasonable?","answer":"No universal requirement follows: total quantity and symmetric two-queue updates already have coarser sufficient representations.","basis":"Preserve positive results."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/figures/queues-counts.png","alt":"961 states; optimal errors 85, threshold-rule errors 240, additional errors 155.","caption":"Artificial enumeration in the source, not a real-world frequency."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/figures/queues-radius.png","alt":"At s=30, the maximum ranges from 15 to 30; midpoint 22.5, radius 7.5, integer-output radius 8.","caption":"Artificial worst-case error in the source."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/figures/queues-dynamics.png","alt":"(m,M) is closed only for the specified symmetric two-queue system; position-specific capacities and three queues can break closure.","caption":"Recalculation of the source’s two counterexamples."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams show classification counts, output radius, and dynamical boundaries; all values were recalculated from the complete artificial state domain.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The source explicitly defines the wholly artificial domain and distribution of 961 states. Exact enumeration and derivation are not a real queue experiment that has been conducted."},"openQuestions":["Actual queue arrivals, services, distributions, and authority are not included in this model."],"taskId":"S-C-9","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.9, Chinese historical source lines 4454–4469","claim_support":["Counting 961 states, 85/240, radius for the maximum, two-queue closure, and two counterexamples."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-binary","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.6, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4414–4431","claim_support":["Optimal majority-decision error and the scalar half-range formula."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.9　双队列的任务差异; Chinese source lines 4927–4942; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/source/S-C-9.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-9/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"835a623388291465d611c513d969acbd470edfc5887c3ea286dfe0bdd5acc78d","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.10":{"title":"C.10 Strict Stability Conditions for Fixed-Delay Feedback","lead":"Feedback having an effect does not mean stability. The strict threshold in this fixed-delay linear model excludes equality and does not prove real-world safety or repair.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4943–4966, original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-10. The current full text is in the theory-v3.0 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","The model is x_{t+1}=x_t−k x_{t−d}, where d is a fixed nonnegative integer, k a fixed real number, and the initial history x_{−d},…,x_0 finite; x is a signed deviation. There is no noise, external input, saturation, or state constraint. The theorem states that the zero solution is asymptotically stable for all initial histories if and only if 0<k<k_c(d)=2sin[π/(4d+2)]. [T-main]","Augment the state to (x_t,x_{t−1},…,x_{t−d}), with characteristic polynomial p(λ,k)=λ^{d+1}−λ^d+k. All roots being strictly inside the unit circle is equivalent to convergence to zero for all initial histories: each mode of the linear recurrence is a power of a root, multiplied by a finite-degree polynomial for repeated roots. If the root modulus is <1, the polynomial times the exponential still decays. A modulus ≥1 provides a nondecaying or growing mode, violating the requirement for all histories. [T-main]","First exclude k≤0: at k=0, root 1 retains a constant mode; at k<0, p(1,k)=k<0, while p>0 for sufficiently large positive λ, so continuity gives a real root >1. For d=0, the sole root is 1−k, and |1−k|<1 directly gives 0<k<2. [T-main]","For d≥1, sufficiently small positive k is stable: the simple root 1 at k=0 moves inside the circle by the implicit-function expansion λ=1−k+O(k²); the other d roots vary continuously from 0 and remain inside for sufficiently small k. Continuity here controls the root set; it does not require each zero root to have the same differentiable expansion. [T-main]","To find boundaries at positive k, set λ=e^{iθ}, 0<θ<2π. From p=0, k=λ^d(1−λ). Since 1−e^{iθ}=2sin(θ/2)exp[i(θ/2−π/2)] and sin(θ/2)>0 on this interval, k=2sin(θ/2)exp{i[(d+1/2)θ−π/2]}. Positive real k requires phase to be an integer multiple of 2π, so θ=(π+4πm)/(2d+1), m=0,…,d. [T-main]","The smallest positive-gain boundary comes from the smallest angle π/(2d+1) and its conjugate angle, giving k_c=2sin[π/(4d+2)]. Roots vary continuously with the parameter and remain bounded at finite parameter values, so leaving the circle requires crossing its boundary. Stability therefore holds from small positive k up to the first boundary. [T-main]","Finding only the first exit is insufficient to prove necessity; restabilization must also be excluded. On the boundary, p_λ=λ^{d−1}[(d+1)λ−d] is nonzero: λ≠0, while (d+1)λ=d would require |λ|=d/(d+1)<1. Thus unit-circle boundary roots are simple and can be differentiated implicitly with respect to k. [T-main]","Using λ′=−1/p_λ and k=λ^d(1−λ), obtain (λ′/λ)=(λ−1)/{k[(d+1)λ−d]}. Take the real part and multiply by the conjugate denominator; the numerator’s real part is (2d+1)(1−cosθ). Hence d log|λ|/dk=(2d+1)(1−cosθ)/{k|(d+1)e^{iθ}−d|²}>0. Every unit-circle crossing at positive k is outward, so the number of exterior roots cannot decrease through inward crossings. There is therefore no region of restored stability at larger gain. [T-main]","At exact equality, a unit-modulus nondecaying mode exists. A floating-point root computed as 0.999999… must not be counted as stability. For k>k_c, a growing mode exists, but an all-zero history remains zero. “Not asymptotically stable for all histories” does not mean every special trajectory must diverge. [T-main]","For the source’s k=0.8 example, the critical values for d=0/1 are 2/1, so both are stable; for d=2/3 they are approximately 0.618033989/0.445041868, so both are unstable. The original guide’s author used numerical characteristic roots for an auxiliary recalculation, obtaining maximum root moduli of approximately 0.2, 0.894427, 1.078049, and 1.129417. This numerical check verifies only these examples and does not replace the strict proof above. [T-main]","With an all-ones initial history, all four cases have the same first step, x1=1−0.8=0.2, showing that feedback has already acted within the model, although convergence properties differ. Even stability does not control transient peaks, costs, human acceptability, or actual repair. Time-varying delay, noise, nonlinearity, or execution constraints require separate analysis; this formula is not a universal recommendation for management gain. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Check assumptions including fixed delay, linearity, no noise, and all initial histories.","Augment the state and use the strict unit-circle root condition.","Exclude k≤0 and prove stability for small positive k.","Find the unit-circle angles and first boundary.","Prove that all positive-k crossings are outward, excluding restabilization.","Use numerics only to assist example checks; distinguish feedback effects, stability, safety, and repair."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"First-step decrease","leftText":"Feedback actually has a present effect.","rightTitle":"Asymptotic stability","rightText":"Requires eventual convergence to zero for every initial history."},{"leftTitle":"Boundary root modulus equals 1","leftText":"A nondecaying mode exists.","rightTitle":"Strictly inside the circle","rightText":"Only then does this model satisfy asymptotic stability for all histories."},{"leftTitle":"Unstable","leftText":"A mode exists that does not converge to zero or grows.","rightTitle":"Every trajectory diverges","rightText":"Special histories such as the all-zero history may still remain zero."},{"leftTitle":"Mathematical stability","leftText":"Long-run convergence to zero in this model.","rightTitle":"Real-world safety and repair","rightText":"Peaks, costs, acceptability, and domain conditions require separate evidence."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the source: with k=0.8, all four delays give first step 0.2, but only d=0/1 are stable.","Teaching counterexample: numerical rounding makes a root modulus slightly below 1, and equality is consequently included in the stability region.","Boundary in the source: the special all-zero history cannot refute instability established by a growing mode.","Boundary in the source: time-varying delays, nonlinearity, and similar changes fall outside the original model’s conditions."],"questions":["Does finding the first unit-circle root prove the complete interval?","Why can the unit-circle root be differentiated?","Why can feedback have an effect while the system remains unstable?","Does apparent convergence over a dozen or so plotted steps constitute a strict proof?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/figures/delay-proof.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.10","start_line":4999,"end_line":5022,"startLine":4999,"endLine":5022}],"outputId":"section-c-10","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Recalculation of the source’s mathematical construction: fixed gain, four delays","text":"At k=0.8, the critical values are 2, 1, 0.618033989, and 0.445041868, respectively, so d=0/1 are stable and d=2/3 unstable. With all-ones histories, all have x1=0.2. Values at step 15 are approximately 0.000000000032768, −0.15989504, −0.83168, and −5.624. Short trajectories do not replace the root criterion.","assumptions":"The source’s fixed deterministic model, with all initial-history values equal to 1; specific approximate values come from independent iteration reported in the guide.","boundary":"Not an experiment on real organizations, nor proof of an infinite-time conclusion from a finite plot."},{"title":"Teaching construction: equality must be excluded","text":"At d=0, k=2, the recurrence is x_{t+1}=−x_t. With x0=1 it yields 1,−1,1,−1… and does not approach zero. At d=0, k=0, it stays at 1. Neither endpoint is asymptotically stable, showing that 0<k<2 must be strict.","assumptions":"Entirely a single-variable linear mathematical model.","boundary":"Establishes no safety threshold for any real controller."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source: with k=0.8, all four delays give first step 0.2, but only d=0/1 are stable.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: numerical rounding makes a root modulus slightly below 1, and equality is consequently included in the stability region.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: the special all-zero history cannot refute instability established by a growing mode.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: time-varying delays, nonlinearity, and similar changes fall outside the original model’s conditions.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does finding the first unit-circle root prove the complete interval?","answer":"Not yet. Reentry at larger k must be excluded; the positive radial derivative completes that step.","basis":"Necessity."},{"question":"Why can the unit-circle root be differentiated?","answer":"Because p_λ is nonzero at the boundary, the root is simple and locally admits implicit differentiation.","basis":"Simple-root check."},{"question":"Why can feedback have an effect while the system remains unstable?","answer":"A first-step effect differs from the long-run root-modulus condition for all histories; delay changes the stability region.","basis":"Levels of effect."},{"question":"Does apparent convergence over a dozen or so plotted steps constitute a strict proof?","answer":"No. The plot illustrates examples only; the all-history conclusion follows from the characteristic-root argument.","basis":"Ceiling of finite computation."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/figures/delay-proof.png","alt":"Stability at small positive k, the first boundary, and outward direction of every crossing yield the strict necessary-and-sufficient interval.","caption":"Based on the complete proof in C.10."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/figures/delay-threshold.png","alt":"At k=0.8, d=0/1 are stable and d=2/3 unstable; equality is excluded.","caption":"Independent recalculation of the source’s formula reported in the guide."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/figures/delay-trajectories.png","alt":"Artificial numerical trajectories for an all-ones initial history, k=0.8, d=0 through 3, and t=0 through 15; all share first step 0.2.","caption":"A finite plot does not replace proof of stability for all times and initial histories."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams give the proof structure, strict thresholds, and numerical trajectories with the same initial history. The trajectories are finite iterations of an artificial model, not purported empirical curves.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section provides a strict proof for a fixed-delay linear difference equation and a mathematical construction from the source; it supplies no facts from real control or social experiments."},"openQuestions":["Transient peaks, costs, disturbances, time-varying delays, and actual execution constraints are not guaranteed by this theorem alone."],"taskId":"S-C-10","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.10, Chinese historical source lines 4470–4493","claim_support":["Necessary-and-sufficient fixed-delay stability proof, strict boundaries, and four examples at k=0.8."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.10　固定时延反馈的严格稳定条件; Chinese source lines 4943–4966; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/source/S-C-10.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"d0df771fc7fcbc343eea13f73a1bf67a7db07e6069e9d112f2fdbc46ff35d342","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.11":{"title":"C.11 Historical Increments and Omissions in Current Representations","lead":"History improving prediction from a coarse representation need not imply an increment beyond the complete current state. Eliminating an additional predictive contribution also cannot negate effects of early experience transmitted through current carriers.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4967–4981, original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-11. The current full text is in the theory-v3.0 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","Retain the 961 equiprobable artificial initial states (a,b)∈{0,…,30}². Each round, each queue serves at most 5 items, with no arrivals: F(a,b)=((a−5)_+,(b−5)_+). “Current” means after round one, and the target is the mean after round two. Do not mistake the initial mean for information after the prediction time, or reset the first-round state distribution to uniform. [T-main]","Compare four inputs: current mean m1; (m1, initial mean m0); complete current ordered two-queue state x1; and (x1,m0). Outputs are real numbers, and loss is expected squared loss under the distribution induced by the given initial-state distribution. This is a different optimization problem from the worst-case absolute error in C.6. [T-main]","Within each input fiber, the target takes values z_i, conditioned using the original initial-state weights. For any output c, conditional squared error satisfies E[(Z−c)²|input]=E[(Z−μ)²|input]+(μ−c)²; the cross term vanishes because E[Z−μ|input]=0. Thus the conditional mean μ is optimal. Weighting by each input’s actual induced probability then gives the global minimum mean squared error. [T-main]","Independent exact recalculation reported in the guide proceeds as follows: enumerate all 31×31 initial states; compute x1=F(x0), x2=F(x1), and target z=sum(x2)/2; group by each of the four input keys; calculate the rational mean of z in each group; sum (z−μ)² for every original initial state in each group and divide by 961. Different original states mapping to the same x1 each still carry probability 1/961; do not deduplicate and assign equal weights anew. [T-main]","The four minimum MSEs are 0.579016561292077, 0.271143397459245, 0, and 0, agreeing with the source’s nine-decimal table. The first two exact rational values are 200877946845133/346929535826874 and 31003222683/114342532304. This is arithmetic on the complete finite model, not estimation from real samples or fitted training performance. [T-main]","Why does the historical mean help? Initial state (0,20)→current (0,15)→next (0,10), with next mean 5; initial state (10,15)→current (5,10)→next (0,5), with next mean 2.5. Both current means are 7.5, but their initial means are 10 and 12.5, respectively. History distinguishes states collapsed by the current mean. Conflicts can remain in other fibers, so adding history lowers MSE without reducing it to zero. [T-main]","The complete current state already determines the next state and target through deterministic F, so minimum MSE is 0. Adding the initial mean cannot improve below 0 and provides no further predictive benefit. This zero result is limited to this model, complete current state, specified target, distribution, and loss; it does not mean every real system is memoryless. [T-main]","Real applications must address measurement, confounding, temporal order, leakage, and model selection. If the current carrier is unavailable altogether, historical increments cannot be said to have been ruled out. The disappearance of a historical predictive increment also does not negate the past’s total effect: if early experiences shape current skills and thereby affect outcomes, the additional predictive contribution disappearing conditional on sufficient current skills limits only the part “beyond the current representation.” [T-main]","This section aligns with the boundary of the historical increment (§4.5): the historical increment concerns historical conditional increments beyond a given current representation, without canceling general learning, carrier formation, or early causation. The artificial deterministic queues are not used to judge people’s trauma, skills, or personalities. The discussion of skills explains a conditional structure, not a diagnosis of a real case. [T-main; T-history]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix the original probabilities of the 961 initial states; do not restore uniformity after propagation.","Advance each state two rounds, using the first-round state as input and the second-round mean as target.","Form fibers for the four inputs separately and use conditional means to minimize squared loss.","Accumulate errors using original weights and check all four MSEs.","Distinguish compensation for omissions in a coarse representation from increments beyond the complete current state.","Real-world extrapolation requires separate checks of measurement, confounding, leakage, and availability of current carriers."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Coarse current mean","leftText":"Collapses the distribution between queues.","rightTitle":"Complete current state","rightText":"Already sufficient to advance the future in this deterministic model."},{"leftTitle":"Historical predictive increment","leftText":"An increase relative to the given current representation.","rightTitle":"History’s total effect","rightText":"May operate through current carriers and does not disappear because the residual is zero."},{"leftTitle":"Uniform initial states","leftText":"Each of the 961 initial states has probability 1/961.","rightTitle":"Uniform subsequent states","rightText":"Reweighting changes the question."},{"leftTitle":"Conditional mean is optimal","leftText":"Expected squared loss.","rightTitle":"Midpoint is optimal","rightText":"Finite scalar worst-case absolute loss; the formulas must not be mixed."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the source: two states with current mean 7.5 have different next means; history can help distinguish them.","Teaching counterexample: retaining only distinct states after propagation, weighting them equally again, and claiming to have recalculated the original MSE.","Boundary in the source: no historical increment after conditioning on the complete current state does not establish that all real systems are memoryless.","Boundary in the source: disappearance of the residual conditional on current skills does not negate the total effect of early experience."],"questions":["Why output the conditional mean in each group?","Why can subsequent states not be given equal weights again?","Does zero MSE mean history had no effect?","Does this table report real algorithm-test performance?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/figures/history-risks.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.11","start_line":5023,"end_line":5037,"startLine":5023,"endLine":5037}],"outputId":"section-c-11","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Recalculation of the source’s artificial model: risks for four inputs","text":"Enumerate all 961 initial states, with at most 5 items served in each of two successive rounds. Using original initial-state weights and conditional-mean decoding, the four risks are 0.579016561292077, 0.271143397459245, 0, and 0. There is no training/test split because the quantity computed exactly is theoretical minimum risk over the complete specified distribution, not predictive performance on an unknown real-world distribution.","assumptions":"A deterministic finite domain, no arrivals, squared loss, and uniform original initial states.","boundary":"These values cannot be claimed as achieved performance of real queue algorithms, nor extrapolated to general human memory."},{"title":"Recalculation of the source’s artificial model: two paths with the same current mean","text":"(0,20)→(0,15)→(0,10), m1=7.5, m0=10, target 5; (10,15)→(5,10)→(0,5), m1=7.5, m0=12.5, target 2.5. m0 provides a distinction; the complete x1 already provides all information needed for advancement.","assumptions":"The two rows are selected from the source’s complete artificial domain without changing the weights of its 961 states.","boundary":"Shows only that this kind of gain can arise from representational omission, not that every gain has that cause."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source: two states with current mean 7.5 have different next means; history can help distinguish them.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: retaining only distinct states after propagation, weighting them equally again, and claiming to have recalculated the original MSE.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: no historical increment after conditioning on the complete current state does not establish that all real systems are memoryless.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: disappearance of the residual conditional on current skills does not negate the total effect of early experience.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why output the conditional mean in each group?","answer":"Squared loss decomposes into conditional variance plus (mean−output)²; setting the latter to zero is optimal.","basis":"Loss-specific proof."},{"question":"Why can subsequent states not be given equal weights again?","answer":"Different subsequent states may have different numbers of initial states mapping into them, so their original probability masses differ.","basis":"Retain the induced distribution."},{"question":"Does zero MSE mean history had no effect?","answer":"It says only that no additional predictive contribution remains given the complete current state, not that history had no role in forming that state.","basis":"Increment versus total effect."},{"question":"Does this table report real algorithm-test performance?","answer":"No. It is theoretical minimum risk over a complete artificial distribution; extrapolation to reality needs separate evidence.","basis":"Identity of the material."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/figures/history-risks.png","alt":"The four minimum MSEs are 0.579016561, 0.271143397, 0, and 0, respectively.","caption":"Exact enumeration of the source’s 961 equally weighted artificial initial states."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/figures/history-paths.png","alt":"Two paths share current mean 7.5 but have different initial means and next targets.","caption":"Artificial paths in the source, not real cases."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/figures/history-boundary.png","alt":"Historical compensation for representational omission is not an increment beyond the complete current state; zero increment does not negate history’s total effect.","caption":"Based on the final paragraph of C.11 and 4.5."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams show risks for four inputs, two specific paths, and the boundary between increments and total effects; values were recalculated by complete rational enumeration reported in the guide.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section explicitly uses an artificial queue model with 961 equally weighted initial states. Independent complete enumeration is not a real experiment or an estimate of human historical effects."},"openQuestions":["Complete current carriers, measurement error, confounding, and leakage for real objects have not been verified."],"taskId":"S-C-11","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.11, Chinese historical source lines 4494–4508","claim_support":["Risks for four inputs, retention of original weights, two paths, and historical boundaries."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-history","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.5, Chinese source lines 620–641","claim_support":["Distinguishing increments beyond a given current representation from historical effects through carriers."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.11　历史增量与当前表示遗漏; Chinese source lines 4967–4981; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/source/S-C-11.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"6bea2f08bab15c1ad4591e32e330bd274e0be94d79f0d750278d3b8772b27a66","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"C.12":{"title":"C.12 Three Boundaries on Combining Local Successes","lead":"Functional composition, causal chaining, and joint resource feasibility are three distinct checks. Knowing that every state has some solution also does not guarantee that current information permits selecting a jointly qualified action.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4982–4997, original-site location /theory/appendix-c#section-c-12. The current full text is in the theory-v3.0 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","This section first separates three kinds of “combination”: whether a transformation preserves the same final answer is a functional condition; whether causal effects can chain through mediators is a joint-response condition; and whether actions can be implemented together within resources is a constraint-set condition. One process may need all three, and a generic “connected” state cannot establish them all. [T-main]","The functional boundary returns to C.7: when q=g∘f, the composition h∘f preserves q only if g is constant on the h-fibers in the reachable intermediate domain. Two steps that separately preserve and count do not guarantee retention of a task about the first position. This is a different failure from the causal or resource obstacles below. [T-main; T-compose]","The source’s causal construction takes equally weighted s=0/1, with m(a)=a(1−s) and y(a,m)=s m. The effect of changing treatment a from 0 to 1 on the mediator is 1−s, respectively 1/0 for the two types, averaging 1/2. At fixed a, the outcome effect of changing mediator m from 0 to 1 is s, respectively 0/1, also averaging 1/2. But the whole-chain effect y(1,m(1))−y(0,m(0))=s(1−s)=0, with mean 0 as well. The product of edge means, 1/4, cannot replace the joint response of 0. [T-main; T-causal]","Different types carrying the two segments explains why unconditional multiplication fails. It does not imply that all chain effects are always zero, or revoke an independently identified total effect. If a real task has directly identified its total effect, retain it according to its grounds rather than requiring recertification through a product of edges. [T-main]","The resource construction has two unit tasks, each exclusively occupying one complete time slot, both due by the end of period one, with capacity 1 in that period. Each can be scheduled alone, but total demand 2>1 makes a joint schedule infeasible. Capacity 2 in the same period permits parallel execution; alternatively, lawfully moving one deadline to period two with capacity 1 there permits sequential scheduling. If both original deadlines remain unchanged, adding period-two capacity cannot fill the original deadline gap. [T-main]","The common feasible action proposition is limited to finite, single-round deterministic tasks. Current observation y is compatible with a nonempty state set X_y, and every state has a qualified-action set A_ok(x). There exists one action selected solely from y that is qualified for every compatible state if and only if the intersection ∩_{x∈X_y}A_ok(x) is nonempty. This guarantees the same action across all compatible states, not secretly selecting different actions according to the true hidden state. [T-main]","Necessity: if a common action a exists, it is qualified for every compatible x, hence belongs to every set and their intersection. Sufficiency: select an a from the nonempty intersection; it is qualified for every compatible x without needing to know which x is real. Each A_ok(x) being nonempty is only necessary: the sets {left}/{right} still have an empty intersection. [T-main]","If both states permit an already authorized preservation action, their intersection can be nonempty while the state remains incompletely identified. Thus full state identification is not a universal prerequisite for every bounded action. However, “preservation” cannot be treated as inherently available: its protections, resources, and authority must first actually be checked into A_ok. [T-main]","Qualified sets must already include task goals, protections, resources, and valid authority. Writing an action mathematically does not establish real eligibility. The conclusion covers only listed compatible states, not a guarantee that unknown states can never appear. Lawful additional observation, waiting, or preservation can change the set of information strategies, but neither happens automatically nor is authorized by this proposition itself. [T-main]","Probabilistic choice under risk tolerance, randomization, and sequential strategies involve different goals and problems. The original proposition asks only for one deterministic action qualified in every state; acceptable average risk cannot quietly replace the original all-state guarantee. Representational sufficiency, computability, accessibility, implementability, resources, and authority to adopt must each be argued for the particular use. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Identify whether the current composition problem concerns functions, causation, or resources, and list the conditions of each separately.","For causation, check same-case joint responses; do not multiply edge means unconditionally.","For resources, check joint scheduling under the same deadlines; late capacity cannot satisfy an earlier deadline.","List the current nonempty compatible-state set and verified qualified-action sets.","Take their all-state common intersection and prove both directions; if empty, do not invent a common action.","Expanded observation or strategies require independently lawful grounds and rechecked goals; retain boundaries concerning unknown states."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Edge means","leftText":"Different types may separately produce edge effects.","rightTitle":"Whole-chain joint effect","rightText":"Requires joint responses within the same types, not direct multiplication."},{"leftTitle":"Individual feasibility","leftText":"Each has a resource schedule separately.","rightTitle":"Joint feasibility","rightText":"Shared capacity and deadlines are satisfied jointly."},{"leftTitle":"Every state has a solution","leftText":"Each action set is nonempty.","rightTitle":"A currently selectable common solution","rightText":"The intersection of all sets is nonempty."},{"leftTitle":"Mathematical action","leftText":"A name is written in a set.","rightTitle":"Real-world qualification","rightText":"Goals, protections, resources, and authorization have been checked."}],"boundaries":["Original causal counterexample: both edge means are 1/2, but the whole chain s(1−s) is 0.","Original resource counterexample: each task is separately feasible, but period-one capacity 1 cannot handle both under the same deadline.","Original counterexample to the common feasible action proposition: {left} and {right} are both nonempty but their intersection is empty.","Boundary in the original: unknown states and randomized sequential strategies are not all covered by a single-round deterministic proposition."],"questions":["Why is a solution in every state insufficient?","Must every microscopic state be recovered before action?","Why might adding capacity in period two be unhelpful?","Can randomization directly count as this proposition’s solution?","Does two effective edges with a zero whole chain negate every total effect?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/figures/joint-causal.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.12","start_line":5038,"end_line":5053,"startLine":5038,"endLine":5053}],"outputId":"section-c-12","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Recalculating the original causal construction: not 1/2 times 1/2","text":"When s=0, the a→m effect is 1, m→y is 0, and the whole chain is 0; when s=1, they are 0, 1, and 0 respectively. With equally weighted types, average edge effects are each 0.5 and their product 0.25, while the actual whole-chain average is 0. No sampling is needed: this is complete algebra for two types.","assumptions":"Specified constructed structural equations, equally weighted types, and binary treatment and mediator contrasts.","boundary":"Does not imply real chains must be zero or revoke independently identified total effects."},{"title":"Recalculating the original resource construction: deadlines cannot be erased afterward","text":"Each of two tasks needs 1 capacity unit and is due in period 1, where only 1 unit is available; demand 2>1 permits no joint plan. Increasing period-one capacity to 2 makes the plan feasible. Lawfully moving one task’s deadline to period 2, with 1 unit there, also works. Adding period-two capacity alone without changing the original deadlines remains infeasible.","assumptions":"Constructed indivisible unit tasks and a single-round plan, with no additional hidden resources.","boundary":"Lawfully changing the deadline is a premise; the model authorizes no real change."},{"title":"Teaching construction: intersections and incomplete identification","text":"Compatible states x1/x2 have action sets {left,preservation}/{right,preservation}; the intersection is {preservation}. Thus already verified qualified preservation can be selected without first distinguishing the states. If preservation lacks resources or authority, remove it from the relevant sets; once they become {left}/{right}, the intersection is empty.","assumptions":"Both states and actions are fictional; “preservation” is qualified only in the first constructed setup.","boundary":"Creates no real authorization for preservation, waiting, information collection, or intervention."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original causal counterexample: both edge means are 1/2, but the whole chain s(1−s) is 0.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original resource counterexample: each task is separately feasible, but period-one capacity 1 cannot handle both under the same deadline.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original counterexample to the common feasible action proposition: {left} and {right} are both nonempty but their intersection is empty.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: unknown states and randomized sequential strategies are not all covered by a single-round deterministic proposition.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why is a solution in every state insufficient?","answer":"The same y alone cannot tell which to choose; a nonempty common intersection is needed.","basis":"The proposition “Common Feasible Actions under Information Constraints.”"},{"question":"Must every microscopic state be recovered before action?","answer":"No. If a verified common action exists, incomplete identification may suffice, but authority cannot be added covertly.","basis":"Common action."},{"question":"Why might adding capacity in period two be unhelpful?","answer":"If both original tasks are due in period one, late capacity cannot meet the original constraint.","basis":"Time conditions."},{"question":"Can randomization directly count as this proposition’s solution?","answer":"No. Probability and risk objectives are analyzed separately according to their own objectives; the original proposition guarantees one action across all compatible states.","basis":"Domain of applicability."},{"question":"Does two effective edges with a zero whole chain negate every total effect?","answer":"No. It rejects only unconditional multiplication of edge means; independently identified total effects remain.","basis":"Causal boundary."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/figures/joint-causal.png","alt":"Two types have edge effects 1/0 and 0/1, each with mean 1/2 but whole-chain effect 0.","caption":"Recalculation of the original constructed structural equations."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/figures/joint-resources.png","alt":"Period-one capacity 1 cannot meet two simultaneous deadlines; lawful rescheduling or added capacity in the same period changes feasibility.","caption":"The original constructed schedule."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/figures/joint-actions.png","alt":"{left}/{right} have an empty intersection; a shared verified preservation action makes the intersection nonempty without full state identification.","caption":"A teaching set example whose premises include real qualification."}],"diagramBrief":"Three diagrams separately show two-type causation, resources under common deadlines, and common-action intersections; the three checks are not conflated.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section consists of constructed two-type equations, unit-task scheduling, and a set proposition; it does not claim validation of real causal experiments, resource demands, or authorization."},"openQuestions":["Actual qualified-action sets, unlisted states, and risk/sequential strategies require separate evidence and authority."],"taskId":"S-C-12","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.12, Chinese source lines 4509–4524","claim_support":["Three composition boundaries, causal and resource counterexamples, F3’s two-direction proof, and eligibility limits."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-compose","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§C.7, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4432–4441","claim_support":["Fiber conditions for preserving identical endpoints under conversion."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-causal","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§2.4.2, Chinese source lines 307–314","claim_support":["The two-type counterexample concerning causal edge means and joint responses."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"C.12　局部成功的三种组合边界; Chinese source lines 4982–4997; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/source/S-C-12.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-C-12/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"031becdb67ec5ececc3cc0f702313e12877b0a0a37074a9f65a4b06cb4dd5a0b","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-c.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-c.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-c.md"};function u(h,e,m,p,f,g){const t=o("SectionLesson");return r(),c("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=n("div",{class:"theory-source"},[n("p",null,[n("a",{id:"part_19"})]),n("h1",{id:"part-title-19",tabindex:"-1"},[l("Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging "),n("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#part-title-19","aria-label":'Permalink to "Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging {#part-title-19}"'},"​")]),n("p",null,"This appendix lists methodological responsibilities that can be shared when different texts are used together. Each text retains its own authoritative definitions and way of being used. The common kernel does not require the same chapter structure, the same number of outputs, or the same numbering. It does not make one text independent empirical evidence for another.")],-1)),i(t,{section:"intro-19-appendix-c"}),e[1]||(e[1]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Method Kernel and Stable References <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.1 Method Kernel and Stable References {#section-c-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A stable reference first locates the relevant level, then returns to the original definitions and conditions; the six-row table does not merge different materials and responsibilities into one kind of evidence.</span></div><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Reference Name</th><th>Content</th><th>Where to Find It</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition layer</td><td>Objects, states, feedback, scales, circles, information status, and recasting</td><td>Framework definitions and authoritative definitions in <em>World Model</em></td></tr><tr><td>Three common method constraints</td><td>Task and representation, evidential status of materials, and facts and eligibility to act</td><td>§4.17</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional formal propositions</td><td>Task recovery, closure, common feasible actions, composition of conversions, error, and dynamic refinement</td><td>§4.18–4.19 and this appendix</td></tr><tr><td>Four empirical questions</td><td>Grouping, channels, historical increments, and representational closure</td><td>Pre-registration of formal tests in Part 4</td></tr><tr><td>Twelve conclusion rules</td><td>Records of outcome statuses, mechanism composition, and bridging to normative procedures</td><td>The individual conclusion rules in Part 4</td></tr><tr><td>Governance invariants</td><td>Finalizing before results, evidence, baselines, authorization, protection, and the life cycle</td><td>Existing governance clauses in both texts</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.1"}),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Common Method Constraints <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.2 Common Method Constraints {#section-c-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Methods 1–3 specify responsible model use; following the procedure does not itself establish a model’s real-world effectiveness.</span></div><p>There are three common method constraints. The first is “Tasks and Representations Must Be Declared Together”: the task, representation, horizon, environment, operations, and error need to be declared together, and static recovery, dynamic closure, and intervention sufficiency need to be tested separately. The second is “The Status of Materials and Scope of Support Must Be Traceable”: the evidential status of materials, source lineage, inferential premises, and scope of support need to be preserved, and reprocessing material does not count as independent empirical evidence. The third is “Facts, Values, and Eligibility to Act Are Established Separately”: facts, value judgments, and eligibility to act need to be established separately. See §4.17 for the full formulation.</p><p>The three constraints specify responsibilities for using models. They do not constitute three empirical laws of the world. Whether they reduce errors or impose unnecessary burdens in real tasks requires separate evaluation. Compliance with the procedures does not establish the framework’s effectiveness.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.2"}),e[3]||(e[3]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Shared Governance Invariants <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.3 Shared Governance Invariants {#section-c-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The nine safeguards protect versions, null conclusions, evidence identity, division of responsibility, parsimony, residuals, lifecycle, and low-power positions. They cannot offset one another through a total score.</span></div><p>There are nine shared governance invariants.</p><p>Invariant one. Finalize the record before results appear, and after they appear only append to it.</p><p>Invariant two. Lack of support for a positive claim does not amount to support for the null model, that is, a comparison model without the claimed factor.</p><p>Invariant three. Definitions, conventions, internal teaching examples, and internal simulations cannot supply external empirical support on their own.</p><p>Invariant four. The evidential status of materials, proposition role, output status, and missing-data status are mutually independent dimensions. Keep them separate.</p><p>Invariant five. A single output cannot automatically satisfy responsibilities for source tracing, modeling, foresight, evaluation, authorization, and execution.</p><p>Invariant six. Model complexity must be subject to simplification, removal of unnecessary elements, and review of necessity suited to the task. Claims of predictive or empirical capability require comparison with a simple baseline and independent evaluation material. Formal proofs, interpretation of texts, and normative arguments are checked according to their own tasks. Inapplicable out-of-sample metrics must not be imposed on them.</p><p>Invariant seven. Residuals generate only competing candidates. They do not automatically generate variables in reality.</p><p>Invariant eight. Capabilities have domains of applicability and validity periods, and are subject to drift, suspension, restoration to a prior state, and retirement.</p><p>Invariant nine. Aggregate results must not erase low-power positions, privacy, withholding for protection, appeal, or exit.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.3"}),e[4]||(e[4]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Preserving Differences Across Texts <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.4 Preserving Differences Across Texts {#section-c-4}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Interoperability must preserve differences. Similar names, similar cases, or complete tables cannot replace actual correspondence between both sides’ definitions and rules.</span></div><p>When the two texts are used together, the following should be checked separately: proposition layers, the pre-registration of tests of the four empirical questions, null conclusions, scale closure, noncommutation tests, and inferential premises; and information status, systems of outputs, unknowns classified by type, experimental evaluation, evidence of capability, and model life cycles. The complete definitions and rules of one text cannot be replaced by something with a similar name, or by a case, from the other. Where the correspondence has yet to be established, keep the differences explicit.</p><p>The framework’s nine scale transformations (§5.5) need not be aligned with the standard transformation steps in <em>World Model</em>. The framework’s scale transformation record, which covers fourteen aspects (§5.7), is also kept separate from the closed system of outputs in <em>World Model</em>. The two share only the minimum meanings of what is preserved, changed, collapsed, or omitted, new unknowns, source lineage, domain of applicability, residuals, and stopping.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.4"}),e[5]||(e[5]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>A Mathematical Example of Bridging Scales <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.5 A Mathematical Example of Bridging Scales {#section-c-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Mathematical bridging must carry objects, mappings, initial values, history, errors, and validity periods. Approximate commutation of one statistic cannot be upgraded into equivalence of entire states.</span></div><p>Consider a microstate x, a coarse-graining map M, microscopic evolution F, and macroscopic evolution F̄. If the difference between M(F_t(x)) and F̄_t(M(x)) remains within tolerance ε over the specified initial conditions, parameter domain, error norm, and time interval, one can discuss approximate commutation for that task. If this holds for only one statistic, it cannot be extended to equivalence of the entire state. A proof must also state the order of limits, how the error is controlled, and the interval of validity. A short-time approximation alone cannot establish a long-term conclusion. This is a general formal example and does not claim to give such a theorem for social objects.</p><p>Mathematical bridging requires carrying the source object, target object, mapping, scaling law or limit, initial values, retained historical information, error, and failure boundary together. It does not require all models to use the same representation, imply that every transfer across scales causes task-relevant loss, or identify losses from social recasting with physical entropy. Establishing a mathematical proof, showing that a real object meets its premises, and deciding which option to adopt in practice are three separate responsibilities.</p><p>Related studies are 05.05 “Gödel, Incompleteness, and Computability,” 05.06 “Effective Theories, Scale Closure, and Coarse-Graining,” and 05.08 “Model Selection, Parsimony, and Expressive Power.” These numbers help locate material. To check a particular mathematical conclusion, return to the assumptions, proofs, and domains of validity in the original works. A bibliography entry cannot substitute for verification.</p><p>See Appendix D for the framework’s map of applications by domain. Each domain retains its terminology, measurements, arguments, and professional boundaries. Teaching examples in the map illustrate conditions and the structure of counterexamples. They do not stand in for experiments that have been carried out or actual cases. Topic numbers connect existing research materials and do not rank theoretical importance.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.5"}),e[6]||(e[6]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Task Recovery and Worst-Case Error (F1) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.6 Task Recovery and Worst-Case Error (F1) {#section-c-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">If states in a representation fiber require different answers, the representation alone cannot recover the task exactly. With error permitted, the answer depends on output space, loss, and distribution.</span></div><p>Let X be a nonempty set of source states, π:X→Y a deterministic representation with Y=π(X), and q:X→V the target. For each y∈Y, the preimage X_y={x:π(x)=y} is called a representation fiber. The decoder may use only y. It must not read the source state, identity labels, or undeclared history behind the scenes.</p><p><strong>Task recovery proposition.</strong> There exists g:Y→V such that q=g∘π if and only if q is constant on each fiber. If it exists, g is unique on the actual image Y.</p><p>Proof. If q=g∘π, the same representation is passed to the same function, so it gives the same target value. Conversely, on each nonempty fiber define its common target value to be g(y). This definition is unambiguous and gives g(π(x))=q(x) for every x. Each y has a source state, so any g satisfying the condition must take that common value. This proposition establishes only the existence of a function between sets. Whether it can be computed on an infinite domain by an algorithm that always halts, and whether a finite budget suffices, are separate questions.</p><p><strong>Finite scalar recovery proposition.</strong> Further suppose X is finite, q is real-valued, the decoder can output any real number, and loss is the worst-case absolute error over the entire domain. Let a_y=min_{x∈X_y}q(x) and b_y=max_{x∈X_y}q(x). Then</p><p>inf_g max_{x∈X}|q(x)−g(π(x))| = (1/2)max_{y∈Y}(b_y−a_y).</p><p>Proof. For any output c, the sum of its distances to the two endpoints is at least b_y−a_y. At least one distance is therefore at least half the range. With c=(a_y+b_y)/2, all target values in the fiber lie between the endpoints, and the maximum distance is exactly half the range. Taking the midpoint in each fiber and then the maximum over all fibers attains the lower bound.</p><p>For a nonnegative tolerance ε, a decoder with error at most ε over the entire domain exists if and only if the range of every fiber is at most 2ε. With an infinite state domain, use suprema and check boundedness. Vector values, restricted outputs, average loss, and probability weighting each give a different optimization problem. Half the range cannot serve as a universal score of information loss across domains.</p><p>Suppose the target is binary, the loss is zero-one loss, and the finite set of source states is uniformly distributed. Let n_y0 and n_y1 be the counts for the two labels in each fiber. The minimum error rate is |X|⁻¹∑_y min(n_y0,n_y1). Only one label can be chosen for each fiber. Choosing the more frequent label attains the smaller of the two error counts. The error under randomized labeling is a convex combination of these two counts and cannot be lower. This expression describes the given distribution. It does not estimate error frequencies in a real population.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.6"}),e[7]||(e[7]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Composition of Conversions and Actual Execution (F4) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.7 Composition of Conversions and Actual Execution (F4) {#section-c-7}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Each step completing its own local task does not guarantee preservation of a common endpoint; representational insufficiency and an incorrect actual rule can also coexist.</span></div><p>Let f:X→Z and h:Z→W, with the final query q already satisfying q=g∘f. The composition h∘f can still recover q if and only if g is constant on every fiber of h restricted to f(X). If q=k∘h∘f, intermediate values corresponding to the same final representation must have the same g value. Conversely, define k by the common g value on each final fiber. The condition concerns only the reachable image f(X). It imposes no constraint on intermediate values without a corresponding source state.</p><p>For example, let X={0,1}². The first stage retains both positions, and the second stores only their sum, while the final query still asks for the first position. The stages individually complete the tasks of storing and counting. Their composition merges (0,1) and (1,0), so it cannot answer the final query. Retaining the Boolean value at the first position repairs this problem without recovering all source details. Changing the final query to counting is simply a different task that can succeed.</p><p>Representational sufficiency and correct execution must also be tested separately. Suppose the representations of three source states are f=(0,0,1), the required answers are q=(0,1,1), and the actual rule is g_actual=(0,0). The first two states require different answers for representation 0, establishing that the representation is insufficient. The third state clearly requires 1 for representation 1, but the actual output is 0. This separately witnesses an independent error in the rule. Changing the rule to (0,1) repairs that error without removing the conflict in representation between the first two states. States for which the output is correct do not lose that correctness because of failures elsewhere.</p><p>This counterexample does not require every real-world fault to be uniquely classified. If the internal representation, input at the time, or actual rule is unknown, retain the gap. The continued existence of an original document cannot substitute for evidence that the reviewer obtained it in time. Being able to consult it after an error differs from having it available as input before the action.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.7"}),e[8]||(e[8]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Closure and Error Propagation (F2, F5) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.8 Closure and Error Propagation (F2, F5) {#section-c-8}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Exact closure requires consistent projected successors for every merged state; approximate closure additionally requires stepwise errors, amplification constants, and valid regions to remain applicable.</span></div><p>Let F:X→X be deterministic one-step dynamics. There exists F̄:Y→Y satisfying π∘F=F̄∘π if and only if π(x)=π(x′) implies π(F(x))=π(F(x′)). The proof follows by replacing the target q in C.6 with π∘F. If the condition holds on the whole domain, then π∘F^t=F̄^t∘π for each nonnegative integer t. The zero-step case is immediate. To go from step t to step t+1, apply the commutation relation once.</p><p>If U is the set of permitted actions and each action has a specified F_u, verify the condition for each permitted action before composing the updates step by step for any action sequence fixed in advance. If action feasibility depends on states that were collapsed, also ensure that a macroscopic action can be executed in all relevant source states in the same fiber, or explicitly limit its availability. If different microscopic implementations are grouped under one macroscopic action, check separately whether they give the same macroscopic result.</p><p>In a finite stochastic system, let P(x,x′) be the transition kernel. If the probability sum ∑_{x′∈B}P(x,x′) for reaching each target block B is the same for every source state in a given block, define the macroscopic transition kernel using that common value. Whatever the conditional distribution of source states within the block given the current macroscopic block and past records, averaging that common value leaves it unchanged. The projected process therefore updates under this macroscopic kernel. If the same macroscopic one-step law is required for all initial distributions, the blockwise equality condition is also necessary. Check it by starting with all mass at each source state in the same block in turn. Retaining only the next-step mean, or obtaining equality by chance for one initial mixture, does not meet this distributional requirement for all initial states.</p><p>Approximate closure also needs a horizon. Let d be a metric on Y. Suppose each reachable x satisfies d(π(F(x)),F̄(π(x)))≤ε, and F̄ satisfies d(F̄(y),F̄(y′))≤L d(y,y′) in the region reached. Starting from y_0=π(x_0), let e_t=d(π(x_t),y_t). The triangle inequality gives e_{t+1}≤ε+Le_t, and the recurrence yields e_t≤ε∑_{j=0}^{t−1}L^j. If the per-step error or L changes over time, adapt the recurrence. The fixed-parameter formula must not still be applied.</p><p>These propositions state exact closure and error propagation under the conditions for approximate closure, respectively. They cannot determine whether an empirical model meets the statewise bound, whether it leaves the working region, or whether an intervention belongs to the original operation set. Closure under the usual distribution, closure under intervention, and normative usability still have to be established separately.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.8"}),e[9]||(e[9]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Different Tasks for Two Queues <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.9 Different Tasks for Two Queues {#section-c-9}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The same mean representation can answer total-quantity questions exactly while remaining insufficient for local overload. Adding the maximum repairs some tasks, but does not guarantee closure under location-specific interventions or higher-dimensional dynamics.</span></div><p>Consider two ordered queues (q₁,q₂)∈{0,…,30}², giving 961 states in total. The local overload target is max(q₁,q₂)&gt;15. For the sole purpose of computing expected error, make the 961 source states equiprobable. This differs from making the means equiprobable and does not imply that real queues follow a uniform distribution.</p><p>If only the mean m=(q₁+q₂)/2 is retained, (0,20) and (10,10) both give m=10, but the overload answers differ. For a sum s from 16 to 30, the same fiber has 2(s−15) overloaded states and 31−s non-overloaded states. The labels of the other fibers are unambiguous. The minimum number of classification errors is therefore ∑_{s=16}^{20}2(s−15)+∑_{s=21}^{30}(31−s)=85, and the error rate is 85/961≈8.844953%. This is the exact count on the complete artificial state domain.</p><p>The rule “report local overload only if the mean exceeds 15” misclassifies 240 states. The 155 errors above the optimal risk for the representation are due to the choice of rule. Those excess errors cannot all be attributed to information loss inherent in aggregation. If the task changes to recovering the maximum queue length, (0,30) and (15,15), both with mean 15, require answers of 30 and 15 respectively. The optimal real-valued output of 22.5 gives a worst-case error of 7.5. If the output must be an integer, the optimal radius for this fiber becomes 8. Output constraints themselves can therefore change the answer.</p><p>Adding the maximum M makes it possible to answer local overload and maximum length exactly, but still does not preserve which position is larger. With exactly two queues, the smaller value is 2m−M. If each position serves at most c tasks per round and no new tasks arrive, then</p><p>M′=(M−c)_+，m′=[(M−c)_++(2m−M−c)_+]/2,</p><p>where a_+=max(a,0). Thus (m,M) has dynamic closure under this symmetric model. When capacity is tied to position as (15,10), the states (0,20) and (20,0) with the same representation become (0,10) and (5,0) respectively. The next representations differ, so closure fails. With three queues, (20,20,5) and (20,15,10) both have mean 15 and maximum 20, but their totals after serving up to 15 queued tasks at each position are 10 and 5 respectively. The two-queue result cannot be extrapolated directly.</p><p>This example retains both positive and negative results. A total can answer a query about the total exactly, and adding a task-relevant variable can repair some shortcomings. The same addition need not work for interventions tied to position, heterogeneous dynamics, or more dimensions. Recovery of all microscopic information need not be a success condition for every task.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.9"}),e[10]||(e[10]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Strict Stability Condition for Feedback With Fixed Delay <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.10 The Strict Stability Condition for Feedback With Fixed Delay {#section-c-10}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Feedback having an effect does not mean stability. The strict threshold in this fixed-delay linear model excludes equality and does not prove real-world safety or repair.</span></div><p>Consider x_{t+1}=x_t−k x_{t−d}, where d is a fixed nonnegative integer, k is a fixed real number, and the initial history x_{−d},…,x_0 has finite values. The variable x is a signed deviation. The equation has no noise, external input, saturation, or state constraints. The zero solution is asymptotically stable for all initial histories if and only if</p><p>0&lt;k&lt;k_c(d)=2sin[π/(4d+2)].</p><p>Expanding the state to (x_t,x_{t−1},…,x_{t−d}) yields a finite-dimensional linear recurrence with characteristic polynomial p(λ,k)=λ^{d+1}−λ^d+k. All characteristic roots lie strictly inside the unit circle if and only if the solution tends to zero for every initial history. The modes associated with repeated roots decay despite their polynomial factors when the root modulus is strictly less than 1.</p><p>At k=0 there is a root at 1, so the system is not asymptotically stable. For k&lt;0, p(1,k)&lt;0, while p(λ,k) is positive for sufficiently large positive λ. There is therefore a real root greater than 1. For sufficiently small positive k, the root at 1 enters the unit circle as λ=1−k+O(k²). The remaining roots, originally at 0, stay inside the circle. Small positive gains are therefore stable. When d=0, the only root 1−k directly gives 0&lt;k&lt;2.</p><p>For positive k, write a boundary root as λ=e^{iθ}, with 0&lt;θ&lt;2π. From k=λ^d(1−λ) we obtain</p><p>k=2sin(θ/2)exp{i[(d+1/2)θ−π/2]}.</p><p>For k to be positive and real, θ=(π+4πm)/(2d+1) with m=0,…,d. The first boundary at positive gain comes from the smallest angle and its conjugate angle, giving k_c(d). The roots vary continuously with the parameter and are bounded at finite parameter values. They must pass through the boundary before leaving the unit circle.</p><p>We must also rule out renewed stability at higher gains. On the unit circle, p_λ=λ^{d−1}[(d+1)λ−d] is nonzero, so boundary roots are simple. Implicit differentiation gives</p><p>d log|λ|/dk = (2d+1)(1−cosθ)/{k|(d+1)e^{iθ}−d|²}&gt;0.</p><p>Thus all crossings at positive k go outward, with no re-entry. Together with stability at small gains, this gives the necessary and sufficient interval stated above. Equality at the boundary leaves a nondecaying mode. A root modulus slightly below 1 in floating-point arithmetic is no basis for including the boundary. Above the boundary a growing mode exists, while the special all-zero history still remains zero.</p><p>With k=0.8 fixed, the critical values for d=0 and 1 are 2 and 1, respectively, and the system is stable. The critical values for d=2 and 3 are approximately 0.618034 and 0.445042, respectively, and the system is unstable. For an all-ones initial history, the first step in all four cases gives 0.2. The feedback term already affects the state in the model, while convergence differs. Even when the system is stable, the result still places no bound on transient peaks or costs and does not establish acceptability to people or real-world repair. Time-varying delays, stochastic disturbances, nonlinearity, and constraints on execution require new analysis.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.10"}),e[11]||(e[11]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Historical Increments and Omissions in the Current Representation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.11 Historical Increments and Omissions in the Current Representation {#section-c-11}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">History improving prediction from a coarse representation need not imply an increment beyond the complete current state. Eliminating an additional predictive contribution also cannot negate effects of early experience transmitted through current carriers.</span></div><p>Use the same 961 equiprobable initial states, but let each queue serve at most 5 tasks per round with no new arrivals. Advance the full state by F(q₁,q₂)=((q₁−5)_+,(q₂−5)_+). The prediction target is the mean after the second round. Compare four inputs after the first round: the current mean, the current mean with the initial mean, the full current state, and the full current state with the initial mean. Under squared loss, outputting the conditional mean of the target for each input fiber attains the minimum expected loss.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Prediction Input</th><th style="text-align:right;">Minimum Mean Squared Error Under the Given Distribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Current mean</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.579016561</td></tr><tr><td>Current mean and initial mean</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.271143397</td></tr><tr><td>Full current state of both queues</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr><tr><td>Full current state of both queues and initial mean</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The calculation advances each of the 961 initial states and retains its original weight. It does not make the states after the first round uniform again. In particular, (0,20) advances to (0,15) and has a next mean of 5. The state (10,15) advances to (5,10) and has a next mean of 2.5. Both current means are 7.5, while the initial means differ. The historical mean helps distinguish differences collapsed by the current projection. The full current state already suffices to determine the future.</p><p>This establishes that a historical predictive increment can arise from an omission in the current representation. It does not establish that real systems universally lack memory. Real data still require attention to measurement, confounding, temporal order, leakage, and model selection. If the full current carrier is unavailable, one cannot claim to have ruled out a historical increment. If earlier experience affects the outcome through a current skill and the residual disappears once that skill is represented sufficiently, the result limits only the additional prediction given the skill. It does not negate the total effect of earlier experience or rule out learning in general.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.11"}),e[12]||(e[12]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-12" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.12</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Three Limits on Composing Local Successes <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.12 Three Limits on Composing Local Successes {#section-c-12}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Functional composition, causal chaining, and joint resource feasibility are three distinct checks. Knowing that every state has some solution also does not guarantee that current information permits selecting a jointly qualified action.</span><details class="xk-gist__codes"><summary>Codes used in this section (1)</summary><ul><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-18-3"><b>F3</b>Common Feasible Actions under Information Constraints</a></li></ul></details></div><p>Whether conversions can compose to give the same final answer, whether causal effects can link into a chain, and whether actions can be jointly executed with available resources concern functions, joint responses, and sets of constraints, respectively. They can occur in the same workflow. A generic status of “connected” cannot make them interchangeable.</p><p>See §2.4.2 for the causal counterexample. The types s∈{0,1} have equal weight, with m(a)=a(1−s) and y(a,m)=sm. The average effect from treatment to mediator and the average effect from mediator to outcome are both 1/2, whereas the effect of the full chain is 0 because different types carry the two effects. This rules out multiplying the means of the separate links unconditionally. It does not withdraw an independently identified total effect.</p><p>The resource counterexample consists of two unit tasks, each taking a full period. Both must be completed before the end of the first period, which has capacity of only 1. Each task is feasible alone, but there is no feasible joint schedule. Joint execution becomes feasible if capacity in that period rises to 2, or if one deadline is legitimately moved to the second period and that period has capacity of 1. If both original deadlines remain, adding capacity in the second period cannot make up for the original deadline. The conclusion follows directly from the stated constraints. It does not certify real-world demand, capacity, or authorization.</p><p><strong>Common feasible action proposition (F3).</strong> For a finite, single-round deterministic task, let X_y be a nonempty observation class and A_ok(x) the set of eligible actions for each state. There exists an action depending only on y that is eligible for every x∈X_y if and only if ∩_{x∈X_y}A_ok(x) is nonempty.</p><p>Proof. If such an action a exists, it belongs to the eligible set of every compatible state and therefore to the common intersection. Conversely, choose any a in the nonempty intersection. It is eligible in every compatible state, and choosing it does not require knowing which state actually holds. Nonemptiness of each state’s eligible set separately is only a necessary condition. For example, if the eligible sets of two states are {左} and {右}, meaning left and right, respectively, each has an option but they share no action. If both allow an authorized preservation action, the intersection can be nonempty while the state remains incompletely identified.</p><p>The eligible sets in the proposition must already incorporate the resources, protections, and valid permissions required by the task. Writing an action in mathematical terms cannot substitute for these real-world conditions. The proposition concerns only the listed compatible states and cannot guarantee that unknown states will never occur. When undertaken lawfully, further observation, waiting, or preservation can change the information and policy sets. None of these occurs automatically. Probability-based choices that tolerate risk, randomization, and sequential policies are analyzed separately according to their own objectives. Representational sufficiency, computability, accessibility, implementability, resources, and authority to adopt must be justified separately for the specific use.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.12"}),e[13]||(e[13]=n("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=s(d,[["render",u]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
